  ('528364394738108063', 'A Mysterious Gentleman Jacob Ravesteyn Online', 'Eddy Habben Jansen', 2026, 'History', 'A Mysterious Gentleman – Jacob Ravesteyn 1844-1910 Eddy Habben Jansen, Utrecht 2026 Jacob Ravesteyn was born in 1844 as the illegitimate son of a young woman from the Dutch farming village of Harmelen. After working as a clog maker and hotel servant in the Netherlands, he entered the service of Edward Adolphus Ferdinand Seymour, Earl St. Maur — the eldest son of the 12th Duke of Somerset — and travelled with him through Europe and North Africa. Following the earl''s sudden death in 1869, Jacob settled in Torquay, Devon, where he eventually became the proprietor of the Union Hotel, a well-known establishment in the heart of the town. Behind this modest success story lies a remarkable historical mystery. Jacob is believed to have been a witness at the secret marriage of Earl St. Maur to Rosina Swan, a union that — if proven — would have made their son Harold the rightful heir to the dukedom of Somerset. In 1924, a London law firm placed an advertisement in Dutch newspapers offering a reward of fifty pounds for any witness or evidence of the marriage. The trail led to Jacob''s relatives in the Netherlands, but proof was never found. Harold''s claim to the title failed, and the mystery was never resolved. This biographical sketch, based on thirty years of research, draws on family letters, archive sources in the Netherlands and England, and personal visits to Torquay and Bournemouth. It tells the story of an unconventional life — shaped by ambition, reinvention, and unanswered questions — that left a small but intriguing mark on British aristocratic history.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/a-mysterious-gentleman-jacob-ravesteyn-online', 'https://archive.org/details/a-mysterious-gentleman-jacob-ravesteyn-online', 'internet-archive:a-mysterious-gentleman-jacob-ravesteyn-online'),
