  ('443226395836873390', 'A New Civic AI Visibility Model for U.S. Domestic Democratic Illumination: The Non-Advisory Civic AI Archivist', 'Christopher Hunt Robertson, M.Ed.', 2026, 'History', 'This prototype proposal contributes to the emerging literature on Civic AI, democratic governance, and digital constitutionalism in four primary ways: 1. Conceptual Innovation: A New Civic AI Category It introduces a distinct category of Civic AI—the non-advisory, non-agentic “Archivist”—whose sole mandate is structured civic memory and visibility. By explicitly separating computational assistance from recommendation, optimization, and decision authority, the proposal clarifies a previously under-developed design space between passive recordkeeping and advisory AI governance systems. 2. Constitutional Admissibility Framework The article shifts analytical focus from AI ethics and performance to democratic admissibility. It argues that systems operating near public authority must first satisfy constitutional criteria—authorization, non-agency, revocability, rights protection, and procedural transparency—before technical implementation questions are addressed. This reframes AI governance as a threshold discipline rather than an engineering exercise. 3. Structural Diagnosis of Digital Conditions It connects three structural pressures—machine-speed consequences, digital invisibility, and the erosion of lived consent—and proposes a bounded visibility instrument as a proportionate response. The argument situates Civic AI within long-standing democratic concerns about memory, accountability, and minority protection, rather than within optimization paradigms derived from commercial AI development. 4. Pre-Design Governance Agenda The proposal advances a structured pre-design question set for legislatures, courts, oversight bodies, citizen panels, and engineers. By foregrounding revocability, degradation pathways, and dismantling as ordinary democratic options, it contributes an actionable research and governance agenda for AI systems that support democratic memory while refusing any role in ruling. Taken together, the article offers an early conceptual model of Civic AI as constitutional infrastructure: a computational instrument that strengthens sightlines around public power while deliberately declining to exercise it. This article proposes a “Non-Advisory Civic AI Archivist” as a deliberately non-agentic civic instrument designed to strengthen democratic memory and procedural visibility in machine-speed digital environments. The Archivist is defined as a visibility tool rather than a decision engine: it preserves structured, time-stamped records of institutional deliberation and, when lawfully requested, generates strictly descriptive analyses of options and impacts without ranking, recommending, or optimizing outcomes. Its authority is archival, not directive. The article outlines four carefully bounded operational modes—Informational, Notary, Continuous Witness, and Analytical—and specifies bright-line prohibitions against person-level profiling, ideological labeling, predictive electoral modeling, enforcement authority, and integration with content-ranking or speech-moderation systems. Rather than offering a technical blueprint, the piece advances a constitutional sketch: any such instrument must be authorized, governed, and revocable under a prior democratic permissions framework that preserves non-delegable human judgment. The central claim is that in an era characterized by machine-speed processing and digital invisibility, democracies increasingly require institutional instruments capable of remembering and witnessing without ruling. Structured civic memory and principled non-agency are presented not as technical enhancements, but as emerging preconditions for maintaining intelligible accountability and meaningful consent over time.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/archivist-proposal-longer-academia.edu-version', 'https://archive.org/details/archivist-proposal-longer-academia.edu-version', 'internet-archive:archivist-proposal-longer-academia.edu-version'),
