  ('865901928231212891', 'A Pragmatic Reading Of The Last Supper, Revolutionary Continuity And The Politics Of Sacrifice', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This study examines the Last Supper through a political and economic lens, analyzing Jesus''s final meal with his disciples as a pragmatic organizing meeting rather than a mystical sacrament. It situates Jesus''s actions within the context of first-century Judea''s Temple extraction economy, arguing that his attack on this system made his execution politically inevitable and that the "body and blood" language represented straightforward instructions for movement continuity after his death. The analysis traces how early Christianity''s political message of economic resistance was systematically depoliticized as the movement transformed from Jewish resistance to imperial religion, with theological reframing obscuring the Last Supper''s original function as organizational preparation for leadership succession under state violence. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/a-pragmatic-reading-of-the-last-supper-revolutionary-continuity-and-the-politics-of-sacrifice', 'https://archive.org/details/a-pragmatic-reading-of-the-last-supper-revolutionary-continuity-and-the-politics-of-sacrifice', 'internet-archive:a-pragmatic-reading-of-the-last-supper-revolutionary-continuity-and-the-politics-of-sacrifice'),
  ('777693071761119505', 'A prayer [manuscript]', 'Hopkins, Samuel, 1721-1803, Boston Public Library. American Revolutionary War Manuscripts Collection', 2025, 'History', 'Holograph', 'https://archive.org/services/img/prayermanuscript00hopk', 'https://archive.org/details/prayermanuscript00hopk', 'internet-archive:prayermanuscript00hopk'),
  ('776577266388453458', 'A Pre 23 Education', 'MPDMedia', 2018, 'History', 'A Pre-23 Education: Home Schooling for Beginners, in Epub Format A First Spanish Reader, by Alfred Remy A Review of Algebra, by Romeyn Rivenburg Botany -- The Science of Plant Life, by Norman Taylor Chamber''s 20th Century Dictionary (in 4 volumes) Eighth Reader, by James Baldwin Elson Grammar School Literature, Book Four First Lessons in Geography, by James Monteith Junior High School Literature, Book 1 Literature (Teacher''s Guide), by Ontario Dept of Education McGuffey''s Eclectic Readers, 1-6, by William McGuffey Modern Geography, by Marion I Newbigin Nature Study (Teacher''s Guide), Ontario Dept of Education Ontario Readers, Fourth Book, by Various Poems Every Child Should Know, by Mary E. Burt Poems Teachers Ask For, by Various School Reading By Grades, 6th, by James Baldwin The Elements of Geology, by William H. Norton The Elson Readers, Book 5 The High School Reader, 1886, by Ministry of Education The Literary World Seventh Reader The Story Book of Science, by Jean-Henri Fabre The Upward Path, by Various There are several free programs for home schooling online; this is a sampling of what you can find among the public domain texts (largely published before 1923, but still good for basic learning) on Project Gutenberg. One free site was called An Old-Fashioned Education, apparently done by a Catholic schoolteacher who spent many hours fashioning the site with hundreds of links to public domain texts separated out into all the basic classes, K-12: grammar, history, science (Origin of the Species notably absent, though a lesser Darwin work was included), arithmetic, fine arts, and so on. It was a noble effort but unfortunately the website has not, as of this date (August 2018), been updated to https protocols and it''s really not safe to click on the links to outdated (sans https) mirror sites of Project Gutenberg, the source of most of the texts. This e-text library is a sort of salute to that teacher''s project -- all of these epubs have been pulled from PG''s safe official site. Math majors will have to look on PG for books on the subject; I only found one work in epub format, a sort of refresher course for algebra students. The rest are all in PDF or Tex format, which I''ve chosen not to include here because I wanted this set to be something easily uploaded to a Kobo or other e-text reader and equally easily-read -- no resizing or formatting needed. There are also many math books here on Internet Archive''s American Libraries, as well as Hathi Trust, and GoogleBooks. I''ve limited this collection to grammar, literature, and science; some of the upper-grade readers have non-fiction works on history as well. You can find full volumes of high school-level history, social studies, fine arts, and practical technology (carpentry, electricity, et al) on PG and IA. Some recommended multi-book sets: America (six volumes), by Joel Cook; The Great Events by Famous Historians (1-14, 17, and 21 -- the rest can be found here on IA); Great Men and Famous Women (8 volumes); The Journal of Negro History (approximately six volumes; more can be found on Hathi Trust); The 1911 edition of Encylopedia Britannica (which, as of yet, only goes up to the ''M''s; Bartleby''s has the rest but beware of pop-up ads). The sequel to this collection is here, in PDF format: https://archive.org/details/a-classic-american-education 50+ PDFs of vintage public domain educational books, from the 1870''s through 1924. These books cover grades K-3, 4-6, 7-8, Teaching, and Miscellany. For modern history, PG also has a collection of State of the Union and Inaugural addresses from Washington to Obama, as well as FDR''s Fireside Chats; several volumes of slave narratives collected in the 1930''s; federal commissions on 9/11 and JFK''s assassination; the Nuremberg War Crimes trials; modern guides to US national parks; US Gov''t papers on scientific study; current world facts &amp; figures via the CIA; and recent achievements by NASA are also available. The most recent works of fiction only go from 1923 up to about 1963, and these are largely pulp science fiction and young adult novel series, like Penny Parker''s mysteries, Dave Dawson''s WWII exploits, and Rick Brant''s science adventures. I''ve included two teaching texts from Ontario, but there are other books on the art of this profession available at the above sources, or simply consult your local brick &amp; mortar library for ideas.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/APre23Education', 'https://archive.org/details/APre23Education', 'internet-archive:APre23Education'),
