  ('1029335938008096363', 'acr 26 bill 20070703 chaptered', 'WebAdmin', 2026, 'History', 'This document contains Assembly Concurrent Resolution No. 26 (ACR 26) , chaptered as Resolution Chapter 67 and filed with the California Secretary of State on July 3, 2007. The resolution formally recognizes the historical significance of former segments of U.S. Highway Route 6 within the State of California and establishes a process for their designation as Historic U.S. Highway Route 6 acr_26_bill_20070703_chaptered . ACR 26 outlines the legislative intent to preserve and highlight remaining publicly maintained portions of the historic highway that once served as a primary transportation corridor and main street for many California communities. The resolution requests that the California Department of Transportation identify eligible segments upon application by local agencies or private entities, determine the cost of appropriate historic highway markers, and install those markers when funding is provided through non state donations acr_26_bill_20070703_chaptered . The text also provides historical context for U.S. Highway Route 6, tracing its origins in the 1920s on the East Coast, its westward expansion to Denver by 1926, and its designation as a transcontinental route reaching Long Beach, California by the late 1930s. It emphasizes the highway’s role in commerce, tourism, and community development, as well as its natural, cultural, scenic, and historic value acr_26_bill_20070703_chaptered This resolution serves as an important primary source for researchers, historians, preservation advocates, and Route 6 enthusiasts documenting California’s legislative efforts to recognize and protect the legacy of one of America’s most storied highways.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/acr_26_bill_20070703_chaptered', 'https://archive.org/details/acr_26_bill_20070703_chaptered', 'internet-archive:acr_26_bill_20070703_chaptered'),
