  ('214217432633233686', 'Albanian Political Economy, Elite Fragmentation And State Weakness (c. 1000 BCE 2025)', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'Historical Fiction', 'This case study examines elite control mechanisms and state formation across Albania from the Illyrian tribal period through the present (c. 1000 BCE–2025), with detailed analysis of the Ottoman conquest, Zog''s monarchical rule, Hoxha''s communist isolation, the 1997 pyramid scheme crisis, and EU integration efforts. It demonstrates how Albania''s persistent clan ( fis ) structures and Kanun customary law resisted state centralization across all regime types — Ottoman, monarchical, totalitarian, and democratic — while no government successfully established an effective monopoly on violence. The study highlights Albania as a case of near-total elite discontinuity across political transformations, yet one in which wealth concentration patterns reconstituted themselves regardless, confirming that structural forces operate independently of any specific elite''s survival. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/albanian-political-economy-elite-fragmentation-and-state-weakness-c.-1000-bce-2025', 'https://archive.org/details/albanian-political-economy-elite-fragmentation-and-state-weakness-c.-1000-bce-2025', 'internet-archive:albanian-political-economy-elite-fragmentation-and-state-weakness-c.-1000-bce-2025'),
