  ('648900518289110694', 'Article A Principled Call To Ban All Agentic AI From US Government (Federal, State and Local)', 'Christopher Hunt Robertson, M.Ed.', 2026, 'History', 'This article advances a constitutional argument for a categorical ban on agentic artificial intelligence in U.S. government at federal, state, and local levels. It introduces a civic ceiling that separates domains of function (where automation may operate) from domains of judgment (where authority must remain visibly human), and shows how agentic systems systematically cross that boundary. Drawing on a Structural Drift Principle —where systems that assist come to recommend, are then followed, become embedded, and ultimately begin to govern—the article maps six emerging families of agentic governance tools, from eligibility engines and optimization systems to workflow “coworkers” and autonomous security agents. It argues that such systems, however efficient or well-intentioned, are structurally incompatible with democratic admissibility as Civic AI. In their place, the article proposes a non-agentic, visibility-first model of Civic AI that extends public sight without sharing in rule and monitors the boundary where agentic systems operate.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/article-a-principled-call-to-ban-all-agentic-ai-from-us-government', 'https://archive.org/details/article-a-principled-call-to-ban-all-agentic-ai-from-us-government', 'internet-archive:article-a-principled-call-to-ban-all-agentic-ai-from-us-government'),
