  ('177864250238924287', 'Attila The Hun, Terror Without Legitimacy', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This case study examines Attila the Hun''s empire (434-453 CE), demonstrating how the most militarily dominant power of Late Antiquity collapsed completely within months of its founder''s death due to the total absence of belief infrastructure. Despite possessing revolutionary cavalry tactics, extracting 2,100 pounds of gold annually in tribute from Rome, and controlling territory from the Rhine to the Urals, the Hunnic Empire vanished so thoroughly that the Huns disappeared as a distinct people within one generation, leaving virtually no archaeological, cultural, or institutional trace. Through systematic comparison with contemporary belief-based empires (Rome, Sassanid Persia) that survived for centuries despite weaker military capacity, this study proves that terror, plunder, and personal charisma cannot create durable power without ideological legitimation, representing the most complete imperial collapse in recorded history. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/attila-the-hun-terror-without-legitimacy', 'https://archive.org/details/attila-the-hun-terror-without-legitimacy', 'internet-archive:attila-the-hun-terror-without-legitimacy'),
