  ('89297973013925116', 'Australian Dictionary Of Biography Volume 19; 1991– 1995 A– Z', 'Melanie Nolan', 2021, 'Biography', 'Volume 19 of the Australian Dictionary of Biography (ADB) contains concise biographies of individuals who died between 1991 and 1995. The first of two volumes for the 1990s, it presents a colourful montage of late twentieth-century Australian life, containing the biographies of significant and representative Australians.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/australian-dictionary-of-biography-volume-19-1991-1995-a-z', 'https://archive.org/details/australian-dictionary-of-biography-volume-19-1991-1995-a-z', 'internet-archive:australian-dictionary-of-biography-volume-19-1991-1995-a-z'),
  ('785003269265222641', 'AUSTRIA, From Habsburg Imperial Aristocracy To Contemporary Social Partnership Capitalism ( 1273 2025)', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'Historical Fiction', 'This comprehensive case study examines elite control mechanisms in Austria across 752 years (1273-2025), tracing how the Habsburg aristocracy adapted through the dissolution of the Holy Roman Empire (1806), collapse of the Austro-Hungarian Empire (1918), Nazi annexation (1938-1945), and post-WWII reconstruction to maintain wealth and influence. It analyzes whether Austria''s distinctive post-1945 social partnership model—characterized by corporatist labor-capital coordination and welfare state expansion—represented genuine power redistribution or elite adaptation to preserve capitalism through managed concessions. The study demonstrates patterns of aristocratic persistence through multiple regime transformations, from imperial absolutism through fascism to contemporary EU-integrated capitalism. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/austria-from-habsburg-imperial-aristocracy-to-contemporary-social-partnership-capitalism-1273-2025', 'https://archive.org/details/austria-from-habsburg-imperial-aristocracy-to-contemporary-social-partnership-capitalism-1273-2025', 'internet-archive:austria-from-habsburg-imperial-aristocracy-to-contemporary-social-partnership-capitalism-1273-2025'),
