  ('505422765733787272', 'Beyond the Reach of Sight: An Ancient Revelation and the Limits of Human Knowledge', 'Ibnu Zulkifli', 2026, 'History', 'For most of human history, the beginning of life was invisible. No microscope. No cellular theory. No understanding of genetics. The womb was a sealed world, and what happened inside it belonged more to speculation than knowledge. Great thinkers tried to explain it. Many were wrong. Some believed life emerged from menstrual blood. Others imagined a fully formed miniature human hidden inside reproductive fluid. These ideas dominated intellectual history for centuries — not because they were correct, but because no one could see deeply enough to challenge them. Then a text emerged in the 7th century. It described human origin not as myth, not as philosophy, but as a staged biological process — beginning from a minute drop, developing through distinct phases, and protected within a secure environment. At the time, there was no scientific pathway to verify such claims. Centuries would pass before technology allowed humans to observe what had always been beyond the reach of sight. So an uncomfortable question remains: When accurate knowledge appears before the tools required to discover it… what is the most rational explanation? Beyond the Reach of Sight is not written to pressure belief. It is written for readers who take questions seriously. This book invites you into a quiet investigation at the intersection of science, history, and intellectual humility. No preaching. No assumptions. Only a question that has echoed across centuries: What if some knowledge does not originate from human observation at all? Read slowly. Question freely. And follow the evidence wherever it leads.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/beyond-the-reach-of-sight', 'https://archive.org/details/beyond-the-reach-of-sight', 'internet-archive:beyond-the-reach-of-sight'),
  ('473874783275571654', 'Beyond the sociology of development', 'Ivar Oxaal, Tony Barnett, David Booth', 1975, 'Economics', 'Beyond the sociology of development by Ivar Oxaal, Tony Barnett, David Booth. A economics book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL11072349W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL11072349W-'),
