  ('761239946657013725', 'Biography of Fakhruddin Ahmed: Statecraft, Crisis And Constitutional Order In Bangladesh', 'S M Nazmuz Sakib', 2026, 'Science', 'The book is a massive academic study of Fakhruddin Ahmed, the technocrat who led Bangladesh''s non party caretaker government from 2007 to 2009. It places his life and work within the broader story of Bangladesh''s struggle to build stable democracy. The author argues that Ahmed is not just a historical figure but a lens through which to examine deep questions about legitimacy, emergency power, and the role of technical expertise in politics. Ahmed was born in 1940 in what was then British Bengal. He was an exceptional economics student at Dhaka University before joining the Civil Service of Pakistan. Later he earned a PhD from Princeton and spent over twenty years at the World Bank. This background gave him a rare combination of technical skill, international credibility, and firsthand knowledge of how weak states actually function. He later became governor of Bangladesh Bank where he introduced major reforms including floating the currency and strengthening banking regulations. Just before the political crisis, he was managing the PKSF, an apex microfinance organization. The political crisis of late 2006 brought Ahmed to power. The country was paralyzed by a standoff between the Awami League and the BNP over who would lead the caretaker government that was supposed to oversee elections. Violence was escalating and the economy was suffering. On the night of 11 January 2007, the military intervened, a state of emergency was declared, and President Iajuddin Ahmed stepped aside as chief adviser. Fakhruddin Ahmed was sworn in the next day. The constitutional basis for his appointment was murky, but the military backed him and the major parties reluctantly accepted him as a neutral figure. The caretaker government ruled for two years under emergency powers, far beyond the ninety day mandate written in the constitution. It launched an enormous anti corruption campaign that arrested over 160 prominent politicians and business figures including both Sheikh Hasina and Khaleda Zia. The campaign was popular at first but raised serious due process concerns. Critics argued it was politically selective and part of a minus two formula aimed at removing both dominant leaders from politics. The government also implemented major institutional reforms that proved more durable. It finally separated the lower judiciary from executive control, a reform ordered by the Supreme Court years earlier but never carried out. It created a biometric national ID system and a new voter list with 81 million registered voters. It reformed the Election Commission and passed over 133 ordinances covering everything from anti corruption law to money laundering prevention. The government''s primary mandate was to hold free and fair elections. In December 2008, Bangladesh went to the polls. International observers judged the elections to be the most credible in the country''s history. The Awami League won a landslide victory and Sheikh Hasina became prime minister. The caretaker government transferred power peacefully and Fakhruddin Ahmed withdrew from public life. But the story did not end there. The new Awami League government quickly moved to entrench its power. In 2011, the Supreme Court struck down the 13th Amendment which had created the caretaker government system. The ruling was widely seen as politically influenced. Parliament then passed the 15th Amendment formally abolishing the caretaker system. Over the next decade, elections became increasingly fraudulent. The 2014 election was boycotted by the BNP. The 2018 election was marred by widespread manipulation. The January 2024 election was so thoroughly controlled that it had no credibility. In July and August 2024, a student led uprising erupted. The protests began over a quota system for civil service jobs but quickly grew into a mass movement against authoritarian rule. More than 800 people were killed. Sheikh Hasina resigned and fled the country. An interim government led by Nobel laureate Muhammad Yunus took over under Article 106 of the Constitution. The Yunus government launched an ambitious constitutional reform process. Eleven reform commissions worked through late 2024 and into 2025. Their proposals were consolidated into the July National Charter of 2025, endorsed by 33 political parties. In February 2026, Bangladesh held simultaneous parliamentary elections and a constitutional referendum. The BNP won a supermajority of parliamentary seats. Sixty one percent of voters approved the July Charter. However the Awami League was largely excluded from the process and the referendum asked voters to approve or reject an 84 proposal package with a single yes or no question. This raised concerns about the quality of popular consent. The post election period immediately generated a new constitutional crisis. The BNP MPs refused to take a second oath that would have bound them to implement the July Charter. This prevented the formation of a Constitutional Reform Council that the Charter required. The BNP argued that its supermajority gave it the authority to implement reforms through ordinary parliamentary processes instead. Litigation challenging the validity of the referendum and the implementation order was filed and the High Court issued a rule nisi. As of March 2026, the constitutional situation remains unresolved. The book also engages deeply with comparative and theoretical material. It examines Pakistan''s doctrine of necessity, Sri Lanka''s 2022 uprising, Nepal''s long constitutional transition, and Latin American anti corruption campaigns like Brazil''s Operation Car Wash. It applies frameworks from democratic consolidation theory, the basic structure doctrine, and thinkers like Bruce Ackerman on constitutional moments, Carl Schmitt on the state of exception, and John Rawls on constitutional essentials. The author argues that the technocratic interlude represented by Fakhruddin Ahmed has both strengths and limits. Technocratic expertise can produce genuine institutional improvements but cannot substitute for democratic legitimacy. The durability of reforms depends on political will and political culture, not just constitutional design. The book concludes that Bangladesh''s constitution remains unfinished. Each constitutional moment from 1972 through 1996 through 2008 through 2024 has tried to settle fundamental questions and each has generated new contests. The future depends on whether the BNP government implements the reform agenda, whether the judiciary develops genuine independence, whether civil society maintains its vitality, and whether political parties learn to accept unfavorable electoral outcomes. The student generation that drove the 2024 uprising carries both the heaviest responsibility and the heaviest hope. Their courage demonstrated that political culture is not fixed. But translating that courage into sustained democratic norms is the great open question.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/biography-of-fakhruddin-ahmed', 'https://archive.org/details/biography-of-fakhruddin-ahmed', 'internet-archive:biography-of-fakhruddin-ahmed'),
