  ('5256285238630402', 'British Malaya, Fragmented Colonialism And Ethnic Segmentation ( 1824 1957)', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This study examines British colonial control in Malaya from 1824 through independence in 1957, analyzing how fragmented administration (three-tier governance structure) and systematic ethnic segmentation (Malays agricultural, Chinese commercial, Indians plantation labor) prevented unified resistance while enabling massive resource extraction through tin mining and rubber plantations. It demonstrates how British authorities suppressed Communist insurgency through forced resettlement of 500,000 Chinese, then negotiated independence with ethnic elite coalitions that preserved colonial economic structures while granting political sovereignty. The case reveals how negotiated decolonization transfers state apparatus to local elites committed to maintaining extraction systems, with ethnic politics and wealth concentration persisting seven decades after independence. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/british-malaya-fragmented-colonialism-and-ethnic-segmentation-1824-1957', 'https://archive.org/details/british-malaya-fragmented-colonialism-and-ethnic-segmentation-1824-1957', 'internet-archive:british-malaya-fragmented-colonialism-and-ethnic-segmentation-1824-1957'),
