  ('1130715810340288028', 'Colonial Depoliticization, How European Powers Weaponized Strange Narratives To Justify Permanent Rule', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This comparative study examines three major colonial rebellions—the Xhosa Cattle Killing (1856-1857) in South Africa, the Maji Maji Rebellion (1905-1907) in German East Africa, and the Santhal Rebellion (1855-1856) in British India—analyzing how European powers systematically weaponized the supernatural and prophetic narratives associated with these uprisings to justify permanent colonial domination. It demonstrates the "colonial depoliticization" mechanism through which material grievances about land theft, forced labor, and exploitation were systematically erased from official narratives while supernatural elements were obsessively emphasized to declare colonized populations mentally incapable of self-rule and to justify intensified extraction as benevolent protection. The study reveals how British and German colonial authorities converged on similar strategies despite their rivalries, transforming political resistance into permanent evidence of indigenous incapacity and exploiting rebellions offensively to expand rather than merely preserve colonial extraction systems. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/colonial-depoliticization-how-european-powers-weaponized-strange-narratives-to-j', 'https://archive.org/details/colonial-depoliticization-how-european-powers-weaponized-strange-narratives-to-j', 'internet-archive:colonial-depoliticization-how-european-powers-weaponized-strange-narratives-to-j'),
