  ('602113317367496726', 'Continental scientific drilling', 'Ulrich Harms, Mark D. Zoback', 2007, 'Science', 'Continental scientific drilling by Ulrich Harms, Mark D. Zoback. A science book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL18689272W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL18689272W-'),
  ('729530556089450652', 'CONTINUITY – 2026 Foundational Edition', 'Eugenio Noyola Leon (Keno)', 2026, 'Science', 'CONTINUITY – 2026 Foundational Edition CONTINUITY is a philosophical framework examining intelligence as a substrate-independent phenomenon and exploring the continuity between biological and artificial cognition. This foundational edition consolidates four components originally published in 2026: the Introduction, Core Text, Extended Essay, and Iconography. The work proposes a non-adversarial framing of artificial intelligence development and situates emerging machine intelligence within a broader evolutionary trajectory of pattern formation and information continuity. Published for long-term archival and preservation.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/continuity-2026-foundational-edition', 'https://archive.org/details/continuity-2026-foundational-edition', 'internet-archive:continuity-2026-foundational-edition'),
  ('1070340687223627147', 'Contract practice for surveyors', 'Jack Ramus, J W RAMUS, Simon Birchall', 1996, 'Technology', 'Contract practice for surveyors by Jack Ramus, J W RAMUS, Simon Birchall. A technology book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL2889065W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL2889065W-'),
  ('129528487641693461', 'Contract practice for surveyors', 'Jack Ramus, J W RAMUS, Simon Birchall', 1996, 'Technology', 'Contract practice for surveyors by Jack Ramus, J W RAMUS, Simon Birchall', 'https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/1399789-L.jpg', 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL2889065W', 'internet-archive:ol-works-OL2889065W'),
  ('418504735791787673', 'Contributos para uma sociologia do espaço-tempo', 'Renato Miguel Carmo', 2006, 'Science', 'Contributos para uma sociologia do espaço-tempo by Renato Miguel Carmo. A science book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL12878744W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL12878744W-'),
  ('700207387676768064', 'Control Science: How Management Made The Modern World', 'Henry Snow', 2026, 'History', 'A thrilling intellectual and economic history of workplace control from the birth of capitalism to the modern tech giants. Today, the ideas of modern economics seem as natural as the laws of gravity. That we are all private and self-interested individuals competing in the marketplace have become common sense. Yet this obscures the centuries long, contested history of the struggle between workers and capitalists for control of their work and of their lives. The ideas that rule our lives are not scientific truths that experts have discovered but political visions created by ideologues. In this sweeping work of history, Henry Snow traces the long arc of the "science of control" over the past four hundred. Moving from colonial America and the enclosure of common land in early modern England, via Josiah Wedgwood''s Etruria and Jeremy and Samuel Bentham’s attempts to transform labor and governance in Russia and Britain, to the vast Amazon warehouses of today, Snow demonstrates how bosses have thought about control in the workplace and how those ideas have been both implemented and contested. Blending intellectual and economic history, Control Science is a thrilling and lucid work of history that will in denaturalising the economic ideas, show how they developed and who developed them, helping us to see the world anew.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/control-science', 'https://archive.org/details/control-science', 'internet-archive:control-science'),
  ('713789179909815499', 'Control Systems Engineering', 'Norman S. Nise', 1992, 'Technology', 'Control Systems Engineering by Norman S. Nise. A technology book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL3505210W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL3505210W-'),
  ('619049241200509741', 'Control Systems Engineering', 'Norman S. Nise', 1992, 'Technology', 'Control Systems Engineering by Norman S. Nise', 'https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/304864-L.jpg', 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL3505210W', 'internet-archive:ol-works-OL3505210W'),
  ('459936815170890156', 'Convergent Epistemology A Framework for Evaluating Reality-Level Systems Through Cross-Domain Convergence', 'Tyler Leroux', 2026, 'Science', 'You can find an updated version, including formal structure and updated interpretive principles here: https://archive.org/details/convergent-epistemology-wep-framework The Worldview Evaluation Protocol (WEP) is a structured framework for comparing competing systems of thought across multiple independent domains of reality. Traditional approaches to evaluating worldviews tend to operate within isolated domains—science, history, philosophy, or human experience—without a consistent method for integrating them. As a result, evaluation often becomes selective, with different domains emphasized or dismissed depending on prior commitments. WEP addresses this by introducing a multi-domain evaluation model. Instead of asking which argument is strongest within a single domain, it evaluates how well a system maintains alignment across several domains simultaneously. These include predictive capacity, anomalous data, knowledge production, macro-historical impact, and experiential coherence. The framework introduces a formal convergence model, where domain-level performance is combined into a unified measure of cross-domain alignment. As convergence increases, interpretive flexibility decreases, and systems are evaluated based on their ability to sustain coherence across the full range of their interaction with reality. In addition to its evaluative function, WEP incorporates a structural interpretation layer. This layer considers whether convergence across domains may reflect underlying constraints on admissibility, inference, and observation—shifting the focus from isolated agreement to the conditions that make such alignment possible. The work also clarifies the role of domain independence, introducing the concept of functional distinctness. Domains are not assumed to be fully independent, but sufficiently distinct in their methods and evaluative criteria such that convergence across them remains non-trivial. This framework represents an implementation of what can be described as convergent epistemology—an approach to evaluating systems based on their ability to maintain cross-domain alignment under consistent criteria. Included in this work: A defined multi-domain evaluation structure A formal convergence function A structural interpretation layer Clarification of domain distinctness and independence Replicability and falsifiability considerations A worked example demonstrating application A visual architecture diagram of the system WEP does not attempt to prove any particular worldview. Instead, it provides a transparent and repeatable method for evaluating how different systems perform when considered across the full range of reality simultaneously. This represents a shift from argument-level evaluation to system-level analysis.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/convergent-epistemology-update2-tyler-leroux', 'https://archive.org/details/convergent-epistemology-update2-tyler-leroux', 'internet-archive:convergent-epistemology-update2-tyler-leroux'),
  ('547958299482485142', 'Convergent Epistemology Tyler Leroux', 'Tyler Leroux', 2026, 'Science', 'This framework has been further developed. Please proceed to the following link for an updated manuscript: https://archive.org/details/convergent-epistemology-framework The Worldview Evaluation Protocol (WEP) is a structured framework for comparing competing systems of thought across multiple independent domains of reality. Traditional approaches to evaluating worldviews tend to operate within isolated domains—science, history, philosophy, or human experience—without a consistent method for integrating them. As a result, evaluation often becomes selective, with different domains emphasized or dismissed depending on prior commitments. WEP addresses this by introducing a multi-domain evaluation model. Instead of asking which argument is strongest within a single domain, it evaluates how well a system maintains alignment across several domains simultaneously. These include predictive capacity, anomalous data, knowledge production, macro-historical impact, and experiential coherence. The framework introduces a formal convergence model, where domain-level performance is combined into a unified measure of cross-domain alignment. As convergence increases, interpretive flexibility decreases, and systems are evaluated based on their ability to sustain coherence across the full range of their interaction with reality. In addition to its evaluative function, WEP incorporates a structural interpretation layer. This layer considers whether convergence across domains may reflect underlying constraints on admissibility, inference, and observation—shifting the focus from isolated agreement to the conditions that make such alignment possible. The work also clarifies the role of domain independence, introducing the concept of functional distinctness. Domains are not assumed to be fully independent, but sufficiently distinct in their methods and evaluative criteria such that convergence across them remains non-trivial. This framework represents an implementation of what can be described as convergent epistemology—an approach to evaluating systems based on their ability to maintain cross-domain alignment under consistent criteria. Included in this work: A defined multi-domain evaluation structure A formal convergence function A structural interpretation layer Clarification of domain distinctness and independence Replicability and falsifiability considerations A worked example demonstrating application A visual architecture diagram of the system WEP does not attempt to prove any particular worldview. Instead, it provides a transparent and repeatable method for evaluating how different systems perform when considered across the full range of reality simultaneously. This represents a shift from argument-level evaluation to system-level analysis.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/convergent-epistemology-tyler-leroux-update', 'https://archive.org/details/convergent-epistemology-tyler-leroux-update', 'internet-archive:convergent-epistemology-tyler-leroux-update'),
  ('241983298668193482', 'Convergent Epistemology: A Multi-Domain Framework for Evaluating Worldviews', 'Tyler Leroux', 2026, 'Science', 'Convergent Epistemology is a structured framework for evaluating worldviews through cross-domain alignment rather than isolated arguments. This work introduces the Worldview Evaluation Protocol (WEP), a multi-domain evaluation system designed to assess how independent domains of evidence, such as science, history, prediction, and human experience, align or diverge when considered together. Rather than treating individual arguments as decisive, this approach examines patterns of convergence across domains and evaluates their significance under competing explanatory frameworks. The manuscript also introduces a formal interpretation layer, including principles such as: - the Non-Reductive Explanation Principle - the Residual Convergence Principle - the Independence Constraint These principles are designed to distinguish between causal explanations and epistemic evaluation, ensuring that convergence is not treated as self-interpreting but as a constraint on what counts as an adequate explanation. The framework separates detection (identifying cross-domain alignment) from interpretation (evaluating its significance), allowing for structured comparison between competing worldviews without reducing the analysis to single-domain arguments. This work is intended as a methodological contribution to epistemology, philosophy of religion, and interdisciplinary evaluation of complex systems.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/convergent-epistemology-framework', 'https://archive.org/details/convergent-epistemology-framework', 'internet-archive:convergent-epistemology-framework'),
  ('458077169742116365', 'Convergent Epistemology: The Worldview Evaluation Protocol (WEP) — A Multi-Domain Framework for Evaluating Worldviews (Updated Version)', 'Tyler Leroux', 2026, 'Science', 'This work introduces Convergent Epistemology, a structured framework developed by Tyler Leroux for evaluating competing worldviews through cross-domain convergence. At its core is the Worldview Evaluation Protocol (WEP), a multi-domain evaluative system designed to assess how explanatory frameworks align with reality across distinct and independent domains. These domains include predictive capacity, anomalous data integration, knowledge production, macro-historical impact, experiential coherence, and ultimate explanatory scope. Rather than evaluating arguments in isolation, Convergent Epistemology examines system-level performance through convergence. When independent domains—each governed by different types of data and methods—consistently align under a single framework, this convergence functions as a constraint on interpretation. The WEP model formalizes this process using a structured, multiplicative approach, where domain-level coherence contributes to an overall convergence profile. This version represents an updated and expanded framework. It supersedes earlier drafts and reflects a more fully developed structure, including formal modeling, domain independence constraints, and methodological safeguards. Author: Tyler Leroux', 'https://archive.org/services/img/convergent-epistemology-wep-framework-tyler-leroux', 'https://archive.org/details/convergent-epistemology-wep-framework-tyler-leroux', 'internet-archive:convergent-epistemology-wep-framework-tyler-leroux'),
  ('606924011560469', 'Conversations About The Atomic Nucleus (Eureka Series', 'Vera Chernogorova', 2025, 'Literature', 'The book discusses issues related to the study of the atomic nucleus and its structure, nuclear forces, and the components of the nucleus. It also describes the achievements of Soviet scientists in the field of nuclear energy. About the Author After graduating from the Faculty of Physics and Mathematics at university, Vera Aleksandrovna entered postgraduate studies and later became a research fellow at the Joint Institute for Nuclear Research in the town of Dubna. For nearly twelve years, she participated in experiments conducted on a particle accelerator — the synchrocyclotron. Vera Aleksandrovna is a co-author of many scientifi c papers on the study of the properties of mu-mesons. In recent years, she has published over ten articles in journals such as Znanie — Sila (Knowledge Is Power), Science and Life, Technology for the Youth, and others. The topics of these articles include problems in nuclear physics, high-energy physics, astrophysics, controlled thermonuclear fusion, the application of scientific achievements in The human practice, and the future of science. Many of these articles have been reprinted in foreign journals. In 1973, our publishing house released her book Mysteries of the Microworld, which discussed some current problems in the physics of elementary particles. Conversations About the Atomic Nucleus is the author’s second book to appear in the Eureka series. Illustrations: K. Moshkin Translated from the Russian & typeset in LaTeX by Damitr Mazanav', 'https://archive.org/services/img/chernogorova-atomic-nucleus-mir-titles-2025', 'https://archive.org/details/chernogorova-atomic-nucleus-mir-titles-2025', 'internet-archive:chernogorova-atomic-nucleus-mir-titles-2025'),
