  ('308073184507150425', 'Cyrus The Great, Strategic Intelligence And The Architecture Of Durable Empire', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This case study examines the Persian Empire under Cyrus the Great (550-330 BCE), demonstrating how strategic intelligence in appropriating existing belief systems, co-opting local elites, and building institutional frameworks created an empire lasting 220 years with administrative influence spanning 1,200+ years. Through systematic comparison with Attila the Hun—who started in nearly identical circumstances but failed to build belief infrastructure or institutions—the study proves that strategic intelligence, not material power or military genius, determines empire durability. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/cyrus-the-great-strategic-intelligence-and-the-architecture-of-durable-empire', 'https://archive.org/details/cyrus-the-great-strategic-intelligence-and-the-architecture-of-durable-empire', 'internet-archive:cyrus-the-great-strategic-intelligence-and-the-architecture-of-durable-empire'),
  ('815475675193611288', 'Czechoslovak Political Economy From Habsburg Bohemia To Contemporary Divergence ( 1526 2025)', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This case study examines elite power mechanisms across 500 years of Czechoslovak political economy (1526-2025), from Habsburg Bohemia through Communist Czechoslovakia to the post-1993 separation into Czech Republic and Slovakia. It analyzes the most egalitarian privatization attempt in history—voucher distribution to all citizens—demonstrating that even this method reconcentrated wealth to 50-55% within 25 years, and uses the Czech-Slovak separation as a natural experiment showing convergence to identical concentration levels (50-55%) despite divergent political paths (liberal democracy versus semi-authoritarian populism). The study tracks multiple complete elite destructions (White Mountain 1620, Beneš Decrees 1945, Communist expropriation 1948) and subsequent reconstitutions, validating the framework''s hypothesis that concentration patterns are structural to capitalism independent of initial conditions or privatization methods. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/czechoslovak-political-economy-from-habsburg-bohemia-to-contemporary-divergence-1526-2025', 'https://archive.org/details/czechoslovak-political-economy-from-habsburg-bohemia-to-contemporary-divergence-1526-2025', 'internet-archive:czechoslovak-political-economy-from-habsburg-bohemia-to-contemporary-divergence-1526-2025'),
