  ('304541604069083923', 'Defining The Question Before Beginning Work A Framework For CEQA, NEPA, And Federal Permitting', 'Tom McMahon', 2026, 'Science', 'Executive Summary International standards bodies have developed rigorous methodologies for defining problems before authorizing technical work. In organizations like the International Telecommunication Union (ITU-T), ISO, and IEC, no standardization effort can begin until a formal "Question" has been posed, justified, reviewed, and approved through multi-stakeholder consensus processes. This document applies these proven principles to environmental and regulatory review processes for Ballona Wetlands reengineering - specifically the California Environmental Quality Act (CEQA), National Environmental Policy Act (NEPA), and federal permitting frameworks. While environmental law contains analogous requirements through mechanisms like "Purpose and Need" statements and "Project Objectives," actual practice often diverges significantly from the rigorous problem-definition standards observed in international technical standards development. Key Findings: Environmental review processes often proceed with inadequately defined project objectives, leading to scope creep, inappropriate alternatives analysis, and regulatory failures. The absence of formal problem validation mechanisms allows technically flawed premises to drive multi-billion dollar infrastructure decisions. Standards bodies'' requirement for explicit justification, resource assessment, and stakeholder consensus before work authorization could prevent many environmental review failures. Case studies demonstrate that projects proceeding without properly defined "Questions" frequently encounter fundamental design errors, legal challenges, and implementation failures. This report proposes a structured framework for improving environmental and regulatory review processes by incorporating the discipline of formal problem definition that has proven successful in international standards development over decades of practice. This requirement is not bureaucratic formalism—it reflects hard-won lessons from decades of collaborative technical work involving governments, industry, and academia across national boundaries. The Question framework ensures: Clear problem definition before committing resources. Explicit scope boundaries to prevent mission drift. Stakeholder consensus on objectives before work begins. Resource justification demonstrating feasibility. Coordination mechanisms to avoid duplicative or conflicting efforts.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/defining-the-question-before-beginning-work-a-framework-for-ceqa-nepa-and-federal-permitting-02', 'https://archive.org/details/defining-the-question-before-beginning-work-a-framework-for-ceqa-nepa-and-federal-permitting-02', 'internet-archive:defining-the-question-before-beginning-work-a-framework-for-ceqa-nepa-and-federal-permitting-02'),
