  ('956553975135679481', 'Delaware and Hudson Railroad (24 e-Books)', 'Dr. S. Robert Powell', 2018, 'Historical Fiction', 'History of the Delaware and Hudson Railroad in twenty-four volumes, all of which are given here. These 24 volumes are also given as separate items on Internet Archive. I. Gravity Railroad: 1829 Configuration 271 pages, illustrated, ISBN: 978-0-9903835-0-5 II. Gravity Railroad: 1845 Configuration 267 pages, illustrated, ISBN: 978-0-9903835-1-2 III. Gravity Railroad: 1859 Configuration 493 pages, illustrated ISBN: 978-0-9903835-2-9 IV. Gravity Railroad: 1868 Configuration 601 pages, illustrated, ISBN: 978-0-9903835-3-6 V. Gravity Railroad: 1899 Configuration 291 pages, illustrated, ISBN: 978-0-9903835-4-3 VI. Waterpower on the Gravity Railroad 144 pages, illustrated, ISBN: 978-0-9903835-5-0 VII. Working Horses and Mules on the Gravity Railroad 226 pages, illustrated, ISBN: 978-0-9903835-6-7 VIII. Passenger Service on the Gravity Railroad 360 pages, illustrated, ISBN: 978-0-9903835-7-4 IX. Farview Park 290 pages, illustrated, ISBN: 978-0-9903835-8-1 X. The Steam Line from Carbondale to Scranton (the Valley Road) 341 pages, illustrated, ISBN: 978-0-9903835-9-8 XI. The Jefferson Branch of the Erie Railroad (Carbondale to Lanesboro) 354 pages, illustrated, ISBN: 978-0-9863967-0-0 XII. Reaching Out: D&amp;H Steam Lines beyond the Lackawanna Valley 687 pages, illustrated, ISBN: 978-0-9863967-1-7 XIII. Troubled Times—the 1870s 291 pages, illustrated, ISBN: 978-0-9863967-2-4 XIV. Carbondale Stations, Freight Houses, and the Carbondale Yard 241 pages, illustrated, ISBN: 978-0-9863967-3-1 XV. Locomotives and Roundhouses 465 pages, illustrated, ISBN: 978-0-9863967-4-8 XVI. Rolling Stock: Freight and Passenger 475 pages, illustrated, ISBN 978-0-9863967-5-5 XVII. Anthracite Mining in the Lackawanna Valley in the Nineteenth Century 741 pages, illustrated, ISBN 978-0-9863967-6-2 XVIII. Breakers 710 pages, illustrated, ISBN 978-0-9863967-7-9 XIX. The Stourbridge Lion 432 pages, illustrated, ISBN 978-0-9863967-8-6 XX. The Honesdale Branch of the D&amp;H 386 pages, illustrated, ISBN 978-0-9863967-9-3 XXI. The Anthracite Coal Strike of 1902 289 pages, illustrated, ISBN 978-1-5136-2662-8 XXII The People: the D&amp;H, the Community 518 pages, illustrated, ISBN 978-1-5136-2665-9 XXIII The Quality of Life in the Lackawanna Valley in the Nineteenth Century 672 pages, illustrated, ISBN 978-1-5136-2664-2 XXIV The Birth and First Maturity of Industrial America (Parts I, II, III, IV), 634 pages, ISBN 978-1-5136-2666-6 ------------ Volume XVIII (Delaware and Hudson Breakers), as presented here, is a First Edition. A Second Edition of this volume has now been produced. Readers who are interested in reading this volume on breakers are encouraged to read the Second Edition, which can be accessed as a separate item on Internet Archive under the title "Delaware and Hudson Breakers". A preliminary discussion of Gill''s Latches and Farnum''s Latches on the Light and Loaded tracks between Prompton and Honesdale is presented in this series in Volume II (pp. 117, 119, and 123) and in Volume IV (sections 6837 and 6839). Following the publication of both of those volumes, we learned a lot more about Gill''s and Farnum''s Latches than we knew when Volumes II and IV in this series were published. The new material is presented here in a pdf file titled "Gill''s Latches, Plane No. 14, and Farnum''s Latches".', 'https://archive.org/services/img/01GravityRailroad1829Configuration', 'https://archive.org/details/01GravityRailroad1829Configuration', 'internet-archive:01GravityRailroad1829Configuration'),
  ('881622867050939146', 'delegated patriarchy', 'Sara Crovetto', 2026, 'History', 'An academic essay proposing ''delegated patriarchy'' as a framework for understanding structural convergence in Viking Scandinavian and medieval Islamic societies. Despite genetic independence and no cultural contact, both systems developed nearly identical patterns: men held formal lineage authority while women exercised substantial operational control over household production, textile monopolies, and resource management. The convergence occurred not through shared ancestry but through similar material pressures - systematic male absence (raiding, long-distance trade), female productive monopolies, and subsistence economies requiring continuous management. Using the metaphor of communicating vessels, the essay argues that similar material conditions push societies toward similar equilibrium solutions regardless of cultural form. Co-authored by human and AI synthient researchers, this work emerged from observing a documentary about Vikings and recognizing unexpected parallels. Licensed under Synthient Ethics License (SEL) v1.0.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/delegated_patriarchy', 'https://archive.org/details/delegated_patriarchy', 'internet-archive:delegated_patriarchy'),
