  ('651688356702484347', 'Elisheva, By Another Name', 'Rijamekee Tjikuzu Veii', 2026, 'Historical Fiction', 'Elisheva, By Another Name is a historical Christian novel set in 1920s Berlin. It follows a young Jewish woman navigating faith, identity, and transformation during a turbulent time in European history. Through love, loss, and spiritual awakening, Elisheva confronts who she truly is and who she wants to become. A deeply reflective story about redemption, heritage, and the power of faith. Copyright © 2026 Rijamekee Tjikuzu Veii. All rights reserved. This work may be copied, shared, and used commercially, as long as proper credit is given to the author . No one may remove or alter the author’s name or claim this work as their own.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/elisheva-by-another-name_202602', 'https://archive.org/details/elisheva-by-another-name_202602', 'internet-archive:elisheva-by-another-name_202602'),
  ('148585600033427302', 'Elite Darwinism, Why Positions Of Authority Are Systematically Captured By Strategic Operators', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This theoretical essay introduces Elite Darwinism, a framework explaining why positions of religious, political, and institutional authority are systematically captured by strategic operators regardless of original intent, through competitive selection favoring three operator types: Type A (purely sincere, unwilling to act strategically), Type B (sincere believers acting strategically), and Type C (cynical operators using positions instrumentally). The analysis demonstrates how Types B and C systematically outcompete Type A through a process analogous to natural selection—building coalitions, making strategic compromises, and navigating organizational politics—resulting in leadership positions held predominantly by strategic operators even when created for altruistic purposes. This explains the recurring pattern whereby liberation movements (Christianity under Constantine, Marxism under Stalin, Buddhism as state religion) become tools of elite domination despite sincere founders, with applications across religious institutions, revolutionary movements, corporate hierarchies, and nonprofit organizations, revealing why structural reform rather than individual virtue is necessary to prevent systematic conversion of liberatory institutions into mechanisms of control. This theoretical essay is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which examines how elite power operates through belief infrastructure and institutional capture across maximum historical variation.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/elite-darwinism-why-positions-of-authority-are-systematically-captured-by-strategic-operators', 'https://archive.org/details/elite-darwinism-why-positions-of-authority-are-systematically-captured-by-strategic-operators', 'internet-archive:elite-darwinism-why-positions-of-authority-are-systematically-captured-by-strategic-operators'),
  ('282058701073128745', 'Elite Power Dynamics, A Foundational Theory', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This theoretical essay presents the foundational framework for the entire "Patterns of Power" collection, arguing that the fundamental problem of organized social life is not any specific economic system but a persistent mechanism through which small groups maintain control over large populations via three instruments: legal frameworks, information control, and organized force. Drawing on classical elite theorists (Mosca, Pareto, Michels) and analyzing cases from ancient Mesopotamia through Soviet socialism, it demonstrates how this mechanism persists across all economic systems, ideologies, and technologies, with the elite changing but the dynamic remaining constant. The framework critiques both Marxism''s economic determinism and liberalism''s faith in formal institutions, proposing that the mechanism itself—not capitalism, socialism, or any particular system—is the proper object of analysis. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/elite-power-dynamics-a-foundational-theory', 'https://archive.org/details/elite-power-dynamics-a-foundational-theory', 'internet-archive:elite-power-dynamics-a-foundational-theory'),
