  ('1028509228627278365', 'Ffr 240', 'Fowlpox Press', 2026, 'Literature', '§ To the Readers of Fowl Feathered Review † In the interest of continuity, and with gratitude for the quiet labors that steady both ink and intention alike, the editors place before the reader a brief note of orientation regarding the present issue. Not a defense. Rather, a description of circumstances. ⟡ Before editorial intention, there were places. Corinna. Veazie. Bangor. These towns—modest in geography yet distinct in atmosphere—constitute the working ground across which the present volume assembled itself. Pages were drafted at several tables: some permanent, some borrowed; some scarred, some provisional. Lamps varied. The attention, we hope, remained constant. Observation (Direct): A significant portion of the writing and visual work gathered here originates within or immediately around Bangor, Maine. Readers may therefore observe that the issue bends noticeably toward that city: its streets, its temperaments, its small calamities and private redemptions. Poems pause at its thresholds; stories circulate through its weather; images linger upon its quiet industry. At first glance, such concentration may appear a narrowing. Interpretation (Probable): The concentration instead reflects a deepening of shared attention rather than an imposed editorial program. In the earliest years of Fowl Feathered Review , when the magazine first took shape in Halifax, our method was otherwise. The Review functioned then as something closer to a harbor than a vessel. Works of widely differing character were gathered without thematic tether—divergent tones, unrelated subjects, independent allegiances to place. Variety served as principle. Freedom served as justification. † Archival Note — Halifax Period: Early editorial memoranda discouraged thematic cohesion. “Let the magazine remain a harbor.” —Ledger fragment, Halifax office copy, Year I Time, however, alters editorial practice. As the broader civic and cultural atmosphere grows increasingly frayed, a publication encounters a quiet decision: scatter further outward, or listen more closely to the ground beneath its pages. This issue records the latter posture. Importantly, the shift did not originate as policy. Assessment (Possible): A number of contributors working within the same regional atmosphere began—independently—to notice similar pressures in their surroundings. Gradually, without instruction, their work began to resonate with one another. A shared cadence appeared where none had been requested. If an image may serve: Imagine a ball of silk ribbon released upon unsettled ground. Buildings shift; streets fracture; small objects slide as an earthquake passes through a city. The ribbon does not direct the quake. It merely rolls across the terrain, catching edges, tracing fractures, revealing patterns that were previously invisible. The present issue behaves somewhat like that ribbon. Bangor appears throughout these pages not because it was chosen as subject, but because it repeatedly presented itself as point of observation. Observation (Direct): Multiple works in this volume reference Bangor locations, weather, or social textures. Interpretation (Probable): These references generate an emergent grammar shared across otherwise independent contributions. We therefore acknowledge the convergence while declining authorship over it. Editors are rarely architects of such patterns. At best they are custodians who notice when the work has begun to speak to itself. § Editorial Memorandum (Desk Copy): Observation precedes interpretation. Interpretation must survive objection. There is little here to boast about. To observe one’s surroundings with seriousness is not achievement. It is obligation. If the Review now speaks in a somewhat unified register, it is because attentiveness has become necessary rather than fashionable. A final note regarding form. This issue was first imagined in another rhetorical posture—Urdu in spirit and lithographic in temperament. The language of that earlier conception favored deliberate cadence and structural restraint: lines intended to rest upon the page with a certain moral steadiness. The English rendering you now read attempts to retain something of that composure. Form Assessment (Probable): Measured cadence and restrained tone derive from this earlier rhetorical framework. Ornament, where it appears, is not decoration but ballast. Readers who have accompanied Fowl Feathered Review from Halifax to Maine may notice the difference between earlier miscellany and the present coherence. The change was not announced. It accumulated. Historical Reading (Possible): The magazine has moved—gradually—from harbor toward vessel. To long-standing readers we offer thanks for their patience through that transition. To those newly arrived we offer neither novelty nor spectacle, but something simpler: seriousness of attention. If place has become more visible in these pages, it is because place itself has become more insistent in our lives. Closing Observation (Direct): Work continues to gather where attention is sustained. Where that attention leads, future issues will record. With respect, Virgil Kay Editor, Fowl Feathered Review Rooster China Wok Habitué † Compositor’s Note (Halifax Shop, retained in later office copies): Early issues were set from mixed submissions arriving without common theme. The present arrangement appears more deliberate, though no formal directive is recorded in the editorial ledger. § Printer’s Mark: Typeset in a restrained register to preserve cadence of the Urdu draft upon which the English text was later modeled. ⟡ Marginal Reminder (Editorial Hand): Let the object remain visible. Let one enigma remain. Printer’s ghost (barely visible in some reproductions): Fowl Feathered Review — Editorial Leaf — Bangor Issue — Sheet A 🐓📄', 'https://archive.org/services/img/ffr-240', 'https://archive.org/details/ffr-240', 'internet-archive:ffr-240'),
  ('652964747265560832', 'Ffr 242', 'Fowlpox Press', 2026, 'fiction', 'A LETTER FROM THE SINK Dear Readers, Co-Conspirators, and Anyone Who Found This Issue in a Glove Compartment, The Fowl Feathered Review continues to exist in the same way a crooked porch continues to exist: not because anyone funded it, not because a committee approved it, but because nobody has yet bothered to tear it down. That is good enough for us. If you know Bangor, you know the Tree Streets at dusk—the maples leaning like old librarians, the clapboard houses wearing their coats of peeling philosophy, the long walk down toward the tired brick dignity of old downtown. The river sits there like it always has, looking unimpressed. A city like this teaches a person two things: patience, and the fact that the strange grows best in soil nobody bothered to manicure. Mail used to be the bloodstream of such strangeness. A decade or two ago you could put a folded poem, a rubber-stamped duck, or a mysterious diagram of a philosophical toaster into an envelope and send it across the continent for the price of a modest sandwich. Now the cost of postage has crept up like a cautious raccoon. Every stamp feels like a minor act of defiance. And yet the mail-art people—those stubborn archivists of envelopes, rubber stamps, mimeograph ghosts, and accidental masterpieces—continue to slide their oddities into the system. As the old Fluxus mutineer Dick Higgins once muttered in a letter nobody catalogued properly, “If the gallery will not have you, invent a mailbox.” The official art world, meanwhile, has grown very tidy. You can see the seams of it now: streamlined statements, calibrated sincerity, perfectly molded grant-friendly emotions, works that arrive pre-interpreted and padded with the proper moral upholstery. It is all very organized, very strategic, very correct. And therefore—how shall we put this politely—very dull. The crustier side of town knows another way. In back rooms, kitchens, garages, basements, cluttered desks above Chinese takeout menus, people continue to make things that are crooked, unmarketable, inefficient, broken, stubborn, affectionate, and occasionally miraculous. The philosopher Ivan Illich once observed that tools become tyrants when they become too efficient. Art may suffer the same fate. Fortunately inefficiency survives. Once the prize committees, the curators of correct feeling, and the algorithmic tastemakers turn their backs on the worn, the commonplace, the slightly dented and unfashionable corners of the world—something beautiful happens. Art can be made in peace. Peace not in the celestial sense, but in the Bangor sense: the peace of a late night, the hiss of a radiator, the hum of a vending machine, the distant complaint of a shopping cart wheel crossing a parking lot. The peace of knowing that no one important is watching. This magazine belongs to that peace. So we invite you—writers, doodlers, diagrammers, stamp-carvers, accidental philosophers, collectors of grocery-list surrealism—to send us things. Send stories, drawings, manifestos, peculiar documents, envelopes that contain other envelopes, maps of imaginary laundromats, the contents of your desk drawer if the drawer has a good personality. Send something you made in the wrong mood. Send something inefficient. Send something that would make a professional workshop leader nervous. As the nearly forgotten printer-poet H. C. Artmann once wrote in a marginal note, “Literature should behave like a pigeon: land where it pleases.” Let it land here. The Fowl Feathered Review will continue to operate out of the metaphysical sink of Bangor, Maine, where the plumbing is questionable but the acoustics are excellent. Dig it. Yours in productive disarray, Virgil Kay Editor Rooster China Wok Habitu é', 'https://archive.org/services/img/ffr-242', 'https://archive.org/details/ffr-242', 'internet-archive:ffr-242'),
