  ('924604283723028433', 'Fowl Feathered Review 247', 'Fowlpox Press', 2026, 'fiction', 'To the Readers of Fowl Feathered Review , There is a moment, just before the page admits you, when the paper seems to hesitate—a faint clank in the margin, a suggestion that something has already passed through here and left its shoes behind—and then, as if to demonstrate, a bubble rises from the pipe, carrying the tableau of a 19th-century typesetter’s error: a single page of Moby-Dick where every “whale” was replaced with “walnut.” The typesetter, fired but unrepentant, later opened a nut shop in Providence. We have tried, as ever, not to tidy that moment away. This issue arrives by improbable routes: damp envelopes ferried across the pond (one still smelling faintly of the Great Tea Flood of 1892, when a warehouse in Boston released a tide of Earl Grey into the streets), a current of Hudson silt caught in the fold, three button boxes from a tag sale whose contents refused orderly catalog, and a handful of singed leaves said—by a man who would not meet the eye—to have slipped briefly from the keeping of a certain Alexandrian library. A bubble drifts by, containing the moment of sale: a shrug, a coin, a miscount that no one corrects. That bubble does not pop. It lingers, slightly to the left of your attention. What matters is not provenance but pressure: the way disparate scraps lean toward one another until a tone emerges—faintly maritime, faintly municipal, faintly singed at the edges—and then, just as it threatens coherence, Squeaky scurries through, popping a bubble of the 1784 Balloon Riot in Paris. The hats of the rioters hang for a moment in the air before settling into the margins, where they remain, inexplicably weightless. You will find no map here, only tracks: some deliberate, some scuffed into being by repetition, some interrupted mid-stride by a silence that refused to be filled. These tracks are not only marks but the record of handling—sorting, lifting, misplacing, setting back down—the quiet labor by which things persist after their instructions have gone missing. Our contributors have written as if the margins might answer back, and occasionally they do. Where the voice grows too pleased with itself, it is quietly set down; where it falters, Larry adjusts the pipe——and once, misjudging the pressure entirely, releases nothing at all. For a moment the page holds. No bubbles. No relief. Only the text, standing without assistance, my sweet lil'' overproofed hinge loaf , and finding it must account for itself. It does not, quite. The silence is longer than intended. Then the pipe resumes, as if embarrassed. We have not annotated the accidents. We have not explained the interruptions. The Pout stands just off the page, arms crossed, watching a bubble of the 1816 Year Without a Summer drift by—inside, a group of poets play cards by candlelight, complaining about the weather. The Pout does not accept the weather. The poets continue anyway. Another figure (you know the one) keeps at the laces that hold the whole contraption together, not to finish the task but to keep it from coming apart all at once. Between them, a kind of balance obtains—not resolution, but continuation. The lingering Alexandrian bubble shifts slightly, as if reconsidering the value of what was lost. No one remarks on it. If the pieces seem to arrive already worn smooth, that is because they have been handled—passed hand to hand, folded, misfiled, rescued, dropped again. A bubble rises, carrying the moment a librarian in 1937 found a love letter tucked into a copy of Ulysses and decided to leave it there. When Squeaky pops it, a sentence escapes—not the letter itself, but a rhythm. You may notice it now, a faint recurrence, a tendency toward looping clauses that do not quite close. This cannot be undone. If they hold together at all, it is by temporary fastenings: habits of touch, small acts of maintenance, the persistence of those who keep sorting even when the system that named the categories has gone quiet. Under that pressure, even a mismatch can briefly take hold, my darling lacquered badger terrine of mild concern , and seem almost intended. We hope you find something here that works on you—not as a collection, but as a small, functioning disorder: a box of mismatched buttons that nonetheless fastens something, somewhere, just long enough for the next movement to begin. A bubble of the 1971 Great Bagel Heist in New York floats into view. It arrives too late, or too early. No one is hungry. It remains anyway, drifting past the Alexandrian one, which does not acknowledge it. The lantern is dim. The gears are cooling. The work is ongoing. Squeaky, somewhere in the dark, gives the pipe a soft, unnecessary rattle. A final bubble rises: inside, a single feather from the hat of a magician who once pulled a live duck from a top hat. The duck quacks. The magician is still looking for it. The other bubble—the one with the shrug and the coin—remains. Sincerely, Virgil Kay Editor, Rooster, China Wok Habitué Keeper of the Bubble Pipe, by Accident P.S. If you listen closely, you can still hear the echo of a bubble from 1912, containing the moment a suffragette in London laughed so hard at a policeman’s hat that he tipped it to her in surrender. The hat is still tipping. The laugh is still echoing. The rhythm persists a little longer than it should. The Pout allows it.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/ffr-247', 'https://archive.org/details/ffr-247', 'internet-archive:ffr-247'),
