  ('1112709251454888853', 'From Athens To The Gulag, The Jacobin Genealogy Of Marxist Leninist Terror', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This study traces the genealogical connection between ancient Greek rationalism, French Revolutionary Jacobinism, and 20th-century Marxist-Leninist totalitarianism, arguing that communist terror represents not a foreign contamination but a radical development within Western civilization''s secular "Athens" tradition. It examines how the French Revolution''s organizational templates—the Committee of Public Safety, revolutionary tribunals, and systematic terror as rational virtue—were consciously adapted by Lenin and transmitted globally through the French Communist Party''s role as ideological incubator for leaders including Pol Pot, Enver Hoxha, Zhou Enlai, and Deng Xiaoping. The analysis demonstrates how Enlightenment rationalism, when unmoored from traditional constraints and claiming absolute authority over human society, produced the gulags and killing fields of the 20th century. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/from-athens-to-the-gulag-the-jacobin-genealogy-of-marxist-leninist-terror', 'https://archive.org/details/from-athens-to-the-gulag-the-jacobin-genealogy-of-marxist-leninist-terror', 'internet-archive:from-athens-to-the-gulag-the-jacobin-genealogy-of-marxist-leninist-terror'),
  ('738677668313840665', 'From Beirut to Jerusalem', 'Thomas L. Friedman', 1988, 'Travel', 'From Beirut to Jerusalem by Thomas L. Friedman. A travel book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL3740421W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL3740421W-'),
