  ('986845205775528792', 'Future Traditions Foundation — Institutional Archive, Year Two: 1999', '''Iolani Pu''u Kolenc', 2026, 'History', 'Creator: ''Iolani Pu''u Kolenc (formerly known as Misa Kelly, Misa Mango-Kelly, Misa Miele Mandigo Kelly, and Annette Marie Puu) Date: 1999 (documents); archived 2026 Subject: Nonprofit arts organizations; Santa Barbara California; contemporary dance; Hawaiian diaspora; cultural equity; women founders; arts administration; positive externality; labor history; arts economics Description: This archive documents Year Two (1999) of the Future Traditions Foundation, a California nonprofit public benefit corporation founded in 1998 and dissolved in 2019. The Foundation was founded and led by ''Iolani Pu''u Kolenc, then known as Misa Mango-Kelly, a choreographer and MFA-trained dancer of Hawaiian diaspora heritage. The documents contained in this archive include: board meeting minutes (January and February 1999), the organizational check register, year-end balance sheet and income/expense statement, a 2019 reflective narrative written by the founder during archival review, press coverage from the Santa Barbara News-Press (March 28, 1999), and a labor valuation analysis establishing the fair market value of the founder''s uncompensated contributions. Primary documentation confirms that the founder served simultaneously as Executive Director, Board Secretary, Program Director, Grant Writer, Compliance Officer, and chief operational staff, contributing approximately 98% of the Foundation''s total labor — as recorded in the May 1999 board minutes — while receiving $20 in personal compensation for the year. The Foundation''s 1999 programming included: umbrella fiscal services to Ajiva Dance Theatre/Plexus; administration of a Santa Barbara County Arts Commission space subsidy that prevented the closure of a local dance studio; the Adopt a Dancer cross-sector mentorship program; the Golden Gifts Program providing complimentary arts access to residents of Transition House and the Banana Bungalow; and production support for the Raga Kings concert at the Unitarian Church. In March 1999, the Foundation was cited by name in the Santa Barbara News-Press alongside the Earth Charter Commission — a body including Mikhail Gorbachev and Maurice Strong — as evidence of Santa Barbara''s capacity for visionary civic work. This archive is uploaded by the founder as an act of historical reclamation. The Future Traditions Foundation''s institutional history as it existed in organizational memory did not adequately reflect the founder''s contributions. This record corrects that absence. The documents are primary sources. The labor was real. The co-founder''s name belongs in the history she built. Her primary co-pilot at this time, with the Foundation, running it day to day, was Sarah Wingren.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/2019-the-future-traditions-foundation', 'https://archive.org/details/2019-the-future-traditions-foundation', 'internet-archive:2019-the-future-traditions-foundation'),
  ('590049809066270168', 'Futureland', 'Walter Mosley', 2001, 'Short Stories', 'Futureland by Walter Mosley. A short stories book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL115562W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL115562W-'),
