  ('74674707341593419', 'Gilbert Munger Web Archive Finding Aid', 'Michael D. Schroeder', 2026, 'History', 'This document is the official archival finding aid and collection guide for "The Art of Gilbert Munger" ( https://gilbertmunger.org ). It provides a scholarly, multi-decade digital catalog raisonné dedicated to the life and work of American landscape painter Gilbert Munger (1837–1903), including access to historical snapshots and period documentation compiled since 1999 .', 'https://archive.org/services/img/gilbert-munger-web-archive-finding-aid', 'https://archive.org/details/gilbert-munger-web-archive-finding-aid', 'internet-archive:gilbert-munger-web-archive-finding-aid'),
  ('845680128261238646', 'Gilberto and the Wind', 'Marie Hall Ets, Teresa Mlawer', 1963, 'Short Stories', 'Gilberto and the Wind by Marie Hall Ets, Teresa Mlawer. A short stories book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL55398W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL55398W-'),
  ('148997404036272645', 'Gilberto and the Wind', 'Marie Hall Ets, Teresa Mlawer', 1963, 'Short Stories', 'Gilberto and the Wind by Marie Hall Ets, Teresa Mlawer', 'https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/104598-L.jpg', 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL55398W', 'internet-archive:ol-works-OL55398W'),
  ('253756728025904254', 'GILGAMEME: Why the First Epic Became the Fittest Survivor of the Ancient Narrative Ecosystem', 'Sum Bunny', 2025, 'Literature', 'GILGAMEME explores the strange evolutionary resilience of the Epic of Gilgamesh , the oldest written story humanity has recovered — and yet one that remains uncannily alive. Why did this epic, out of countless lost narratives, become the fittest survivor ? This essay reframes Gilgamesh not as a literary relic, but as a memetic organism : a story capable of mutation, selection, transmission, and adaptation across 4,000 years of cultural change. From clay tablets to classroom syllabi, from myth cycles to psychological archetypes, Gilgamesh persists because it contains structural traits that replicate well , including: a dual-hero architecture, a death-awareness catalyst, a recursive quest form, a psychologically universal confrontation with mortality, and an elegant paradox: Gilgamesh attains immortality not within the story, but through the story’s survival. The essay argues that cultural evolution follows patterns analogous to biological evolution — and that some stories survive not because of institutional protection, but because their memetic genome is built to endure. Includes a final “Free Flash” artwork remixing an Assyrian relief into a recursive, mandala-like cosmogram — a visual metaphor for story-survival through repetition. Released with full Creative Commons freedom (CC BY 4.0) as part of the Ark Hive Press open-access catalog.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/gilgameme-sum-bunny-2025', 'https://archive.org/details/gilgameme-sum-bunny-2025', 'internet-archive:gilgameme-sum-bunny-2025'),
