  ('385169143209548094', 'Hawai''i, From Indigenous Kingdom To American State (c. 1400 2025)', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This comprehensive four-part case study examines Hawai''i''s transformation from an independent Polynesian kingdom (c. 1400-1893) through American territorial status (1898-1959) to statehood and the present (1959-2025), analyzing how indigenous dispossession, plantation capitalism, and political absorption created extraction patterns that persist despite democratic transformation. It traces how the Great Māhele land reforms (1848), sugar plantation expansion, contract labor immigration, the 1893 overthrow, and eventual annexation systematically transferred Hawaiian lands and sovereignty to American interests while reducing Native Hawaiians from 400,000-800,000 at contact to a marginalized minority. The study demonstrates how extraction systems can emerge gradually through legal mechanisms, market forces, and demographic catastrophe rather than immediate military conquest, and examines whether statehood has broken or perpetuated these patterns into the 21st century. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/hawaii-from-indigenous-kingdom-to-american-state-c.-1400-2025', 'https://archive.org/details/hawaii-from-indigenous-kingdom-to-american-state-c.-1400-2025', 'internet-archive:hawaii-from-indigenous-kingdom-to-american-state-c.-1400-2025'),
