  ('589765481746370059', 'Herbert Haley Biography 2026 02 16', 'Michael Briggs', 2026, 'History', 'A biography of Herbert "Herb" Haley (1918-1970), a prominent early 20th-century broadcaster and amateur radio operator . The document traces his journey from an orphaned youth in Milton-Freewater, Oregon, through his early work in the Civilian Conservation Corps, Amateur Radio, and his work in broadcast radio and early TV . Key highlights include his wartime career at KGO in San Francisco, where he anchored the "11th Hour News" and served as a producer for Pacific War coverage for the ABC network . It details his service as a Merchant Marine "Sparks" (radio operator) in the South Pacific aboard the SS Flying Arrow and SS Cape Ann . + The biography also covers his post-war entrepreneurial ventures in Ohio, where he co-founded the Champion City Broadcasting Company and discovered a young Rod Serling . A significant portion of the document is dedicated to his lifelong passion for amateur radio, documenting his various callsigns (W7ELX, W9CDT, W8CDT, and W6VBY) and his achievements, including the "Worked All Zones" award.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/herbert-haley-biography-2026-02-16', 'https://archive.org/details/herbert-haley-biography-2026-02-16', 'internet-archive:herbert-haley-biography-2026-02-16'),
