  ('1066664109072708720', 'Home at Elkton Oregon - part 4 of 4', 'Jeffrey Hunt', 2026, 'History', 'This is Entry 4 of 4 from “A Simple Sketch of the Life of Mrs. Anna Elizabeth Mandler Wells,” transcribed by Mary T. Wells. This final installment moves from the day-to-day realities of early settlement on the Umpqua—food scarcity, isolation, and the steady work of building a life—into the larger crises and turning points that shaped the region, including the Scottsburg flood of 1861 and the hard winter of 1861–62. It also follows the growth of the family and the hard-won creation of schooling and community life on the frontier. Included are family photographs and a short reflection that places the memoir in the hands of the person who preserved it: Mary T. Wells, the youngest daughter and family chronicler, shown both as a young woman and later in Elkton with her sister Flora at the boarding house they kept near the bridge. Here we are again just Flora + Mary With an empty house to call a home and empty hearts as well as lonely ones', 'https://archive.org/services/img/elkton-house-mary-and-flora-wells-00-new', 'https://archive.org/details/elkton-house-mary-and-flora-wells-00-new', 'internet-archive:elkton-house-mary-and-flora-wells-00-new'),
