  ('380234135648996681', 'Hungarian Political Economy From Magyar Migration To Contemporary Illiberal Democracy ( 896 2025)', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This comprehensive outline examines Hungarian elite adaptation and wealth concentration across 1,129 years (896-2025), tracking patterns through Magyar conquest, Mongol devastation, 150-year Ottoman tripartition, Habsburg domination, Treaty of Trianon territorial losses, Holocaust and Nazi occupation, complete communist expropriation (1945-1989), and rapid post-communist reconcentration under Orbán''s illiberal democracy. Unlike the Austrian case study which demonstrated elite family continuity across transformations, the Hungary study reveals that while the landed aristocracy (Esterházy, Károlyi, Batthyány families) was completely destroyed during communist rule, wealth concentration patterns returned from 5-10% to 45-50% within just 36 years (1989-2025), suggesting structural features of capitalism generate inequality independent of specific elite survival. The outline details research design for tracking the Three Monopolies (wealth, violence, information) across five major parts covering foundation through contemporary Orbán-era oligarch creation. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/hungarian-political-economy-from-magyar-migration-to-contemporary-illiberal-democracy-896-2025', 'https://archive.org/details/hungarian-political-economy-from-magyar-migration-to-contemporary-illiberal-democracy-896-2025', 'internet-archive:hungarian-political-economy-from-magyar-migration-to-contemporary-illiberal-democracy-896-2025'),
