  ('685371171856887946', 'Jordan, Stability Through Poverty A Mandate Successor State Case Study', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'Historical Fiction', 'This case study analyzes the Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan from its creation as a British mandate territory in 1921 through 2025, examining how an economically marginal, ethnically divided state achieved unusual stability through authoritarian minority rule, permanent external subsidy dependence, and systematic exclusion of its Palestinian majority from political power. It demonstrates how tribal-military patronage networks and strategic irrelevance paradoxically protected the kingdom from the catastrophic conflicts that destroyed wealthier neighbors. The study reveals a foundational contradiction: Jordan survived not by building inclusive institutions or achieving economic self-sufficiency, but by institutionalizing East Bank Bedouin dominance over Palestinian majorities through mechanisms remarkably consistent across one hundred four years and multiple regional transformations. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/jordan-stability-through-poverty-a-mandate-successor-state-case-study', 'https://archive.org/details/jordan-stability-through-poverty-a-mandate-successor-state-case-study', 'internet-archive:jordan-stability-through-poverty-a-mandate-successor-state-case-study'),
