  ('985392142709430712', 'Lebanon, From Famine To Failed State How French Confessional Engineering Created A Century Of Catastrophe ( 1920 2025)', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This case study examines how French mandatory rule in Lebanon (1920-1946) engineered a confessional political system—distributing power by religious sect based on the 1932 census—that would determine Lebanon''s trajectory through a brief golden age, catastrophic civil war (1975-1990), Syrian occupation, and eventual total state collapse (2019-2025). It traces how France created Greater Lebanon by annexing Muslim-majority territories to Mount Lebanon, established sectarian quotas for all government positions (Maronite president, Sunni prime minister, Shiite speaker), and froze these ratios based on a single census that was never repeated. The study demonstrates how this constitutional sectarianism—designed by France to divide Lebanon and prevent unified opposition—made civil war nearly inevitable when demographic shifts and Palestinian refugees destabilized the delicate sectarian balance, and how the same structures enabled elite looting that produced economic collapse killing 80% into poverty by 2025. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/lebanon-from-famine-to-failed-state-how-french-confessional-engineering-created-', 'https://archive.org/details/lebanon-from-famine-to-failed-state-how-french-confessional-engineering-created-', 'internet-archive:lebanon-from-famine-to-failed-state-how-french-confessional-engineering-created-'),
