  ('114871400911474804', 'Letters from India (1583–1588) — Filippo Sassetti, translated into English', 'Filippo Sassetti (author); Ettore Marcucci (editor, 1855)', 2026, 'Literature', 'First complete English translation of the India letters (Letters LXXVII–CXI) of Filippo Sassetti (1540–1588), from Ettore Marcucci''s scholarly edition, Lettere edite e inedite di Filippo Sassetti (Florence: Le Monnier, 1855). Sassetti was a Florentine merchant, scholar, and member of the Accademia degli Alterati who lived in Cochin and Goa from 1583 until his death. These 32 letters, plus two supplementary letters by contemporaries, describe the Portuguese Estado da India, the pepper and spice trade, Indian religions and customs, encounters with Brahmin scholars, and — most famously — the structural similarities between Sanskrit and European languages, an observation predating Sir William Jones by two centuries. The translation preserves Marcucci''s editorial footnotes, textual apparatus, and original document structure. Translated with the assistance of Claude (Anthropic) and reviewed against the Italian source text. The original Italian edition was digitized by the Internet Archive from a copy held by the Getty Research Institute (archive.org/details/lettereediteeine00sass).', 'https://archive.org/services/img/sassetti_india_letters_english', 'https://archive.org/details/sassetti_india_letters_english', 'internet-archive:sassetti_india_letters_english'),
