  ('582641485800809176', 'Liberation Day, A Future History Of 2026', 'Dan Svyetlo', 2026, 'History', 'Liberation Day is a work of political satire in the form of a fictional history of 2026. Written in January 2026 shortly before ICE agents killed 2 citizens in Minnesota, it imagines what might happen soon based on political, economic, technical and environmental trends to that point, and explores how people might react to these developments. There are numerous short descriptions of sex and violence. This work is entirely fictional. Readers may share this for free; no permission needed.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/liberation-day-a-future-history-of-2026', 'https://archive.org/details/liberation-day-a-future-history-of-2026', 'internet-archive:liberation-day-a-future-history-of-2026'),
  ('513329895053461769', 'Liberia, A Case Study In Recycled Dispossession How The Architecture Of Slavery Was Rebuilt By The Enslaved', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This study examines the Republic of Liberia from its founding by the American Colonization Society in 1816 through 2025, analyzing how African American settlers who had experienced slavery and racial exclusion in the United States constructed a settler colonial state in West Africa that replicated those same mechanisms of exclusion against the indigenous majority. It traces 133 years of Americo-Liberian governance (1847-1980) during which approximately 5% of the population governed 95% through systems including the hut tax, forced labor, dual legal structures, and resource extraction agreements like the Firestone concession, followed by the catastrophic collapse into two civil wars (1989-2003) that killed 250,000 people and the subsequent reconstruction efforts. The case study demonstrates how settler colonialism operates as a structural mechanism independent of the racial identity of those who administer it, connecting the founding dispossession at Cape Mesurado in 1821 directly to contemporary governance challenges including the 2024 war crimes court proposal and unresolved questions of accountability. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/liberia-a-case-study-in-recycled-dispossession-how-the-architecture-of-slavery-w', 'https://archive.org/details/liberia-a-case-study-in-recycled-dispossession-how-the-architecture-of-slavery-w', 'internet-archive:liberia-a-case-study-in-recycled-dispossession-how-the-architecture-of-slavery-w'),
