  ('654112802749929430', 'Listen to the Edge: Threshold Technology After Objects', 'Sum Bunny', 2026, 'Science', 'Listen to the Edge is a work of technological philosophy exploring how advanced knowledge systems change when they encounter physical and conceptual thresholds that cannot be occupied—only approached. Using the idea of “threshold regimes” (such as extreme, unstable physical limits exemplified by speculative superheavy elements), the essay argues that future technologies will increasingly shift from extraction and control toward sensitivity, calibration, and listening. Drawing analogies from semiconductor physics, precision instrumentation, and critical phenomena, it reframes technology as the practice of engineering boundaries rather than exploiting materials. The essay also introduces a critique of survivorship bias embedded in scientific taxonomies like the periodic table, suggesting that stable substances represent only what endured—not the full landscape of what is physically possible. In place of object-centered progress, it proposes a regime-based understanding of science and technology, where restraint, retreat, and epistemic humility become technical skills. This essay is a conceptual companion to AFTER 147: The Antikythera Shift , which examines extreme physical thresholds as sites where matter transitions from possessing properties to enacting processes. Listen to the Edge extends that analysis outward, exploring how such thresholds reshape the philosophy of technology itself—shifting emphasis from objects and extraction toward sensitivity, calibration, and epistemic restraint.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/listen-to-the-edge-sum-bunny-2026', 'https://archive.org/details/listen-to-the-edge-sum-bunny-2026', 'internet-archive:listen-to-the-edge-sum-bunny-2026'),
