  ('870036901750822722', 'Lowell A Concise History Howe', 'Howe, Richard P., Jr.', 2026, 'History', 'Lowell: A Concise History provides a sweeping narrative of Lowell, Massachusetts—the city that served as the "Silicon Valley" of the 19th-century Industrial Revolution. Once a quiet farming village, Lowell was transformed by the power of the Merrimack River and the ingenuity of the textile industry into a global powerhouse that attracted world leaders and defined American industrialization. This book chronicles the city’s entire journey: from its explosive growth in the 1820s and the critical roles of innovation and immigration, through the long economic decline of the post-war years and the Great Depression, to its modern-day resurgence. Written by local historian Richard P. Howe Jr., this concise volume explores how the same forces of persistence and experimentation that built the "Spindle City" continue to propel its reinvention today. It is an essential resource for anyone interested in labor history, urban planning, and the enduring story of the American immigrant experience in one of the country''s most dynamic post-industrial cities.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/lowell-a-concise-history-howe', 'https://archive.org/details/lowell-a-concise-history-howe', 'internet-archive:lowell-a-concise-history-howe'),
