  ('1131295831952350326', 'Ma''at And The Living God, Divine Kingship, Temple Wealth, And Three Thousand Years Of The World''s Oldest Belief Infrastructure', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This case study examines elite power mechanisms in Pharaonic Egypt across the full three-thousand-year span from unification under the first dynasty (c. 3100 BCE) to the Roman annexation in 30 BCE, analyzing how the divine kingship ideology, the concept of ma''at, and the temple economy functioned as interlocking instruments of political authority and wealth extraction. It traces critical transformations including the Old Kingdom pyramid labor system, the Middle Kingdom''s democratization of the afterlife, the New Kingdom''s temple accumulation crisis, and Akhenaten''s failed attempt to replace the entire theological infrastructure — the most thoroughly documented ancient case of an elite invention attempt and its collapse. The case demonstrates both the extraordinary longevity achievable when a belief system is built on deeply pre-existing foundations and the structural tendency of interpretive classes to accumulate institutional authority until they rival the rulers they were designed to serve. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/maat-and-the-living-god-divine-kingship-temple-wealth-and-three-thousand-years-o', 'https://archive.org/details/maat-and-the-living-god-divine-kingship-temple-wealth-and-three-thousand-years-o', 'internet-archive:maat-and-the-living-god-divine-kingship-temple-wealth-and-three-thousand-years-o'),
