  ('622745598983900574', 'Medallions: The Art of Politics and Generosity', 'Dumbarton Oaks', 2025, 'History', 'Medallions are among the most exquisite examples of Roman art from late antiquity surviving today. Made from precious metals and distributed as gifts at imperial celebrations, they demonstrated an emperor’s generosity at moments of personal, political, and military triumph. Their images and inscriptions acted as propaganda to bolster the imperial regime and advertise its strength, success, and virtue. To receive a medallion, often from the emperor’s own hand, was a mark of social status and imperial favor. Medallions became treasured objects, publicly displayed by their recipients in their homes and on their person set into ornate jewelry. They conveyed messages about their owner’s status, prestige, and place in the Roman hierarchy. Medallions: The Art of Politics and Generosity explores how these objects sat at the intersection of art, celebration, politics, and status and what they can tell us about the Roman empire at the end of the classical world.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/dumbarton-oaks-medallions-exhibition', 'https://archive.org/details/dumbarton-oaks-medallions-exhibition', 'internet-archive:dumbarton-oaks-medallions-exhibition'),
