  ('1056158159070077459', 'Norway, From Viking Kingdom To Petroleum State A Case Study In Elite Power And Social Democracy ( 872 2025)', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This comprehensive case study examines Norwegian political and economic development across 1,153 years (872-2025), analyzing elite power, wealth concentration, and labor organizing from Viking unification through the petroleum era. Unlike Switzerland''s elite continuity, Norway experienced dramatic elite discontinuity through the Black Death (50-60% mortality), Danish subordination (1380-1814), and Nazi occupation (1940-1945), yet wealth concentration persisted across regime transformations. The study documents Norway''s distinctive path: late independence (1905), hydroelectric industrialization creating concentrated working-class communities, the strongest labor movement in Western Europe (50-60% union density peak), universal suffrage for both genders simultaneously (1913—among world''s earliest), DNA (Labor Party) governance for 30+ years, and petroleum discovery (1969) creating world''s largest sovereign wealth fund ($1.5 trillion by 2024).', 'https://archive.org/services/img/norway-from-viking-kingdom-to-petroleum-state-a-case-study-in-elite-power-and-so', 'https://archive.org/details/norway-from-viking-kingdom-to-petroleum-state-a-case-study-in-elite-power-and-so', 'internet-archive:norway-from-viking-kingdom-to-petroleum-state-a-case-study-in-elite-power-and-so'),
