  ('759956551766286151', 'On The Nature Of Things', 'Erik Jorgensen', 2026, 'Science', 'This book is a modern reworking inspired by De Rerum Natura by Lucretius. Although much ancient philosophical literature has been lost to time, Lucretius’ poem itself survives in an incomplete manuscript tradition, having narrowly escaped disappearance before its rediscovery in the early fifteenth century. Written over two millennia ago, the work presents a poetic synthesis of Epicurean philosophy, itself rooted in Democritus’ atomism: the doctrine that all things consist of atoms moving in a void. While the destruction of ancient libraries—most notably those associated with Alexandria—contributed to the loss of many classical texts, Lucretius’ work endured through later copying rather than continuous transmission. Since antiquity, science has independently rediscovered and vastly expanded upon several of the core ideas anticipated by ancient atomism. However, modern physics has profoundly revised these notions. Today, two principal theoretical frameworks dominate fundamental science: the standard model of cosmology and the standard model of particle physics. Although neither corresponds directly to the atomism of antiquity, both echo its central insight—that nature is governed by universal principles acting on fundamental constituents within spacetime. This work revisits Lucretius’ vision in light of modern scientific understanding, offering a contemporary reinterpretation rather than a reconstruction of the ancient doctrine. This book is a work of science and natural philosophy. Its purpose is to describe the physical universe using empirically grounded models, mathematical structure, and testable principles. In doing so, it necessarily limits itself to questions that can be addressed by observation, experiment, and logical consistency. Claims concerning morality, ultimate purpose, divine intention, and the afterlife fall outside the scope of scientific inquiry as practiced here. Such claims are neither affirmed nor denied by this work; rather, they are recognized as belonging to philosophical, theological, or cultural traditions that operate according to different standards of meaning and justification. Readers should therefore not interpret the absence of supernatural explanations as a polemic against faith, but as a methodological boundary intrinsic to science itself. The cosmological narrative presented in this book—including its discussion of the Big Bang—serves as a scientifically inspired account of origins, not a metaphysical or theological one. It is offered as a model of physical genesis grounded in contemporary physics, without making claims about ultimate causation, divine agency, or moral law. Readers from religious traditions are invited to approach this work in the spirit of dialogue rather than conflict: as an exploration of how the universe behaves, not a declaration of how one ought to live or what one must believe. Where science is silent, this book remains silent. Where science speaks, it does so without intent to offend, persuade, or replace personal faith.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/on-the-nature-of-things_20260405', 'https://archive.org/details/on-the-nature-of-things_20260405', 'internet-archive:on-the-nature-of-things_20260405'),
  ('1136625293591429374', 'On The Nature Of Things Edit 07', 'Erik Jorgensen', 2026, 'Science', 'This book is a modern reworking inspired by De Rerum Natura by Lucretius. Although much ancient philosophical literature has been lost to time, Lucretius’ poem itself survives in an incomplete manuscript tradition, having narrowly escaped disappearance before its rediscovery in the early fifteenth century. Written over two millennia ago, the work presents a poetic synthesis of Epicurean philosophy, itself rooted in Democritus’ atomism: the doctrine that all things consist of atoms moving in a void. While the destruction of ancient libraries—most notably those associated with Alexandria—contributed to the loss of many classical texts, Lucretius’ work endured through later copying rather than continuous transmission. Since antiquity, science has independently rediscovered and vastly expanded upon several of the core ideas anticipated by ancient atomism. However, modern physics has profoundly revised these notions. Today, two principal theoretical frameworks dominate fundamental science: the standard model of cosmology and the standard model of particle physics. Although neither corresponds directly to the atomism of antiquity, both echo its central insight—that nature is governed by universal principles acting on fundamental constituents within spacetime. This work revisits Lucretius’ vision in light of modern scientific understanding, offering a contemporary reinterpretation rather than a reconstruction of the ancient doctrine. This book is a work of science and natural philosophy. Its purpose is to describe the physical universe using empirically grounded models, mathematical structure, and testable principles. In doing so, it necessarily limits itself to questions that can be addressed by observation, experiment, and logical consistency. Claims concerning morality, ultimate purpose, divine intention, and the afterlife fall outside the scope of scientific inquiry as prac