  ('261809654084121975', 'Pranayama; From The Cave To Yoga', 'Da Silva', 2026, 'History', 'Here is the presentation text for Internet Archive , specifically tailored for an American English-speaking audience. It uses academic and descriptive language suitable for long-term archiving and research. Title: Pranayama; From the Cave to Yoga — At the Origins of Spiritual Consciousness Description: This essay explores the prehistoric roots of spirituality and prāṇāyāma (the science of breath) through the dual lenses of neuro-archaeology and the phenomenology of the breath . The text transports the reader 16,000 years into the past, into the heart of the decorated caves of the Upper Paleolithic . It posits that the technical gesture of the shaman—spraying pigments from the mouth in a state of hypercapnia and trance—represents the foundational act of human religiosity. Key themes include: Biological Evolution: The impact of the domestication of fire on the development of the cranial brain in relation to the enteric brain. Phenomenology of the Breath: The lived experience of the breathing body as a tool for creation (parietal art) and an entry point to the sacred. The Missing Link: The connection between Stone Age shamanic practices and the yogic traditions of the Indus Valley. Comparative Religion: Parallels between the "breath of life" in Genesis and the ritual use of psychoactive substances ( gañjā , peyote, ayahuasca, and fly agaric). This work offers a unique perspective at the intersection of anthropology, evolutionary biology, and Eastern philosophy, redefining yoga not as a late invention, but as the formalization of a millenary respiratory experience.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/pranayama-from-the-cave-to-yoga', 'https://archive.org/details/pranayama-from-the-cave-to-yoga', 'internet-archive:pranayama-from-the-cave-to-yoga'),
