  ('563147044497918188', 'Projected El Niño (2026)', 'Dr. James Hansen', 2026, 'Science', 'Crossing Earth''s Rubicon ▪ White paper by the climate research scientist Dr. James Hansen - published on March 20th, 2026. Abstract: ▪ Models are converging on prediction of an El Niño beginning this year, peaking in early 2027. ▪ El Niño strength and frequency are important, especially the issue of whether these are modified by global warming . ▪ However, the more important knowledge that needs to be extracted from near-term global warming concerns the interpretation of ongoing, extraordinary, acceleration of ocean surface warming .', 'https://archive.org/services/img/projected-el-nino-03-20-2026', 'https://archive.org/details/projected-el-nino-03-20-2026', 'internet-archive:projected-el-nino-03-20-2026'),
  ('664030752781659637', 'Projected Global Temperatures (2025)', 'Dr. James Hansen', 2025, 'Science', '89 Seconds to Midnight ▪ White paper by the climate research scientist Dr. James Hansen - published on April 15th, 2025. Abstract: ▪ Global temperature for 2025 should decline little, if at all, from the record 2024 level. ▪ Absence of a large temperature decline after the huge El Nino-spurred temperature increase in 2023-24 will provide further confirmation that IPCC’s best estimates for climate sensitivity and aerosol climate forcing were both underestimates . ▪ Specifically, 2025 global temperature should remain near or above +1.5°C (+2.7°F) relative to 1880-1920, and, if the tropics remain ENSO-neutral, there is good chance that 2025 may even exceed the 2024 record high global temperature.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/projected-global-temperatures-04-15-2025', 'https://archive.org/details/projected-global-temperatures-04-15-2025', 'internet-archive:projected-global-temperatures-04-15-2025'),
  ('1141855134570472692', 'Projects', 'Rakshit', 2026, 'Science', 'CS Projects', 'https://archive.org/services/img/projects_202603', 'https://archive.org/details/projects_202603', 'internet-archive:projects_202603'),
