  ('1080308292114280923', 'ROMANIA, From Ottoman Suzerainty To European Oligarchy', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This case study examines elite power and wealth concentration in Romania across 314 years (1711-2025), from Ottoman suzerainty and Phanariote Greek rule through independent kingdom, fascism and Holocaust, Communist dictatorship under Ceausescu, violent December 1989 revolution, and post-Communist oligarchic consolidation. It analyzes how concentration followed predictable capitalist patterns (48-52% by 2025) despite unique factors including externally-imposed elites, extreme Communist repression, violent revolution (unlike peaceful Czech transition), persistent poverty as EU''s poorest member, and endemic corruption—demonstrating that capitalism structurally generates ~50% concentration regardless of initial conditions, revolution type, or absolute wealth levels. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/romania-from-ottoman-suzerainty-to-european-oligarchy', 'https://archive.org/details/romania-from-ottoman-suzerainty-to-european-oligarchy', 'internet-archive:romania-from-ottoman-suzerainty-to-european-oligarchy'),
