  ('1031463209931859880', 'Sacred Fragmentation, Divine Authority And Decentralized Elite Control In The Holy Roman Empire And Classic Maya City States', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'Classic Literature', 'This comparative study examines how the Holy Roman Empire (962-1806 CE) and Classic Maya city-states (250-900 CE) independently developed identical political architectures despite zero contact or cultural exchange. It analyzes how both civilizations created decentralized systems of sacred authority—divine rulers (Emperor and K''uhul Ajaw) serving as cosmic intermediaries, interpretive classes (bishops and priests) monopolizing sacred knowledge, competitive elite networks maintaining fragmentation through shared ideology, and wealth extraction framed as religious duty. The convergence demonstrates that elite power mechanisms are structural solutions to recurring political problems rather than cultural inventions, providing the strongest possible evidence that similar conditions produce similar power structures regardless of cultural content. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/sacred-fragmentation-divine-authority-and-decentralized-elite-control-in-the-hol', 'https://archive.org/details/sacred-fragmentation-divine-authority-and-decentralized-elite-control-in-the-hol', 'internet-archive:sacred-fragmentation-divine-authority-and-decentralized-elite-control-in-the-hol'),
