  ('958629054366654353', 'Salzburger Klima', 'Peter Siegfried Krug', 2026, 'History', 'This 46-page comprehensive documentation (available in German and English) provides a comparative biographical analysis of institutional abuse and societal indifference in Salzburg, Austria, between 1966 and 1983. The research contrasts the life of U.S. author Dave Pelzer ( A Child Called ''It'' ) with the lived experiences of Peter Siegfried Krug across various institutions, including the infant home in Itzling, the Pro Juventute children''s village in Guggenthal, and the private foster home in Zanderstraße 5. The core of the study examines the "Salzburger Klima"—a specific cultural-historical phenomenon of "looking away" (structural ignorance) driven by a conservative-Catholic consensus and the remnants of "Black Pedagogy." The document details systemic failures in the Austrian educational, police, and medical systems, such as the misdiagnosis of trauma-induced suffocation as epilepsy. Furthermore, it explores Resilience through the intellectual sublimation of Chess Composition (blindfold problem composition) and the therapeutic role of nature as survival mechanisms against overwhelming structural violence. This record serves as a mandatory historical documentation to ensure the findability and permanence of victim testimony on platforms like Zenodo, Academia.edu, and Archive.org.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/salzburger-klima', 'https://archive.org/details/salzburger-klima', 'internet-archive:salzburger-klima'),
