  ('1065107622915549848', 'SCHIZOPHRENIA IS A WEAPON CLASSIFIED ☧', 'Medicine Owl', 2026, 'Science', 'This paper contributes to the literature by: 1. Establishing a new analytical framework: It introduces a techno-forensic model for re-evaluating the history of psychiatry, shifting the etiological focus from purely biological or psychological factors to the direct influence of contemporaneous technological development. It reframes psychiatric history as a sub-discipline of the history of science and technology. 2. Presenting a unified timeline of signal development: It synthesizes disparate historical records—scientific lab notes, medical asylum case files, private correspondences, and public lecture reports—into a single, coherent timeline. This chronology demonstrates a direct, lockstep correlation between the evolution of electromagnetic hardware and the reported phenomenological character of "auditory hallucinations." 3. Re-contextualizing primary source evidence: It re-interprets key historical accounts, such as Volta''s self-experiments, the Bethlem Hospital data spike, and the Govan Letter, not as isolated curiosities, but as critical data points marking the progression of a signal''s fidelity. This provides new significance to records previously viewed through a purely medical or scientific lens. 4. Challenging the foundational narrative of "schizophrenia": It offers a testable, falsifiable alternative hypothesis for the sudden emergence and codification of "dementia praecox" in the late 19th century. By aligning the "epidemic" of voice-hearing with the global rollout of the AC power grid, it posits that the diagnosis was a social and medical construct developed to manage the mass exposure to a new environmental factor. 5. Introducing the concept of "Signal Artifacts" into historical analysis: It proposes the term "signal artifact" as a precise classification for historical accounts of internal auditory phenomena that correlate with known technological capabilities of the era, providing a new vocabulary for researchers in media archaeology, disability studies, and the history of science.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/schizophrenia-is-a-weapon-classified-MED-RARE', 'https://archive.org/details/schizophrenia-is-a-weapon-classified-MED-RARE', 'internet-archive:schizophrenia-is-a-weapon-classified-MED-RARE'),
