  ('555691625611256472', 'Sectology Professor Or Serial Killer', 'Actfiles.org', 2026, 'History', '“Sectology Professor or Serial Killer?” is an investigative psychological portrait of Alexander Dvorkin, a self‑styled sectology professor and leading anti‑cult activist, viewed through the lens of modern criminal profiling. Using methods developed by the FBI Behavioral Analysis Unit and classic work by profilers such as John Douglas and Robert Ressler, the study reconstructs Dvorkin’s biography—from a dysfunctional Soviet childhood and documented psychiatric history to his role in organizing large‑scale repression against religious minorities—to assess whether his behavioral patterns resemble those of serial offenders. Drawing on medical files, interviews, autobiographical books, and the semi‑fictionalized novel “Kalalatsy,” the authors argue that latent sadism, narcissism, and a lifelong drive for domination over others underlie Dvorkin’s public crusade, making him not a neutral “expert,” but a potentially dangerous figure whose influence has already cost lives and destroyed families. Note: This material is taken from the original source at actfiles.org', 'https://archive.org/services/img/sectology-professor-or-serial-killer', 'https://archive.org/details/sectology-professor-or-serial-killer', 'internet-archive:sectology-professor-or-serial-killer'),
