  ('318547008562072943', 'Seventy pioneer women translators', 'Lebert, Marie', 2025, 'History', '[ web version - part one ] [ web version - part two ] After being anonymous or hidden behind a male pseudonym, women began to sign their translations with their real names in the 16th century and have been prominent translators for five centuries. Most of them were also writers. They were literary figures, scientists and political thinkers. Many of them were trailblazers for women’s rights, and fought for gender equality and education for all women. These short biographies were written by Marie Lebert with the help of Wikipedia. The second part ("Thirty trailblazing women writers and/or translators", from page 48) offers longer biographies. A French version is available in " Une histoire de la traduction en 120 portraits " (second part). A Spanish version is available in " Una historia de la traducción en 120 retratos " (second part).', 'https://archive.org/services/img/women-translators', 'https://archive.org/details/women-translators', 'internet-archive:women-translators'),
