  ('295228919577654046', 'Sudan''s Islamic Turn, Elite Appropriation Of Belief And The Fracturing Of A Nation ( 1956– 2011)', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This case study examines Sudan''s Islamization from independence (1956) through South Sudan''s secession (2011), analyzing how President Nimeiry''s 1983 September Laws—imposed as a transparently political bargain with Hassan al-Turabi''s Muslim Brotherhood amid economic collapse—appropriated centuries of embedded Sufi Islamic identity to legitimate elite state capture. It traces how the National Islamic Front''s systematic tamkeen purges, oil extraction strategy, jihad narrative, and suppression of alternative interpretations (including the execution of reformer Mahmoud Mohammed Taha) created a self-reinforcing kleptocratic system that fractured the nation, produced two genocidal campaigns in the south and Darfur, and ultimately unraveled through South Sudan''s secession, the 2019 revolution, and the catastrophic 2023 civil war. The case is notable for operating under modern documentary conditions where the evidentiary record—including direct economic data, the Nimeiry-Turabi quid pro quo, and al-Turabi''s own strategic writings—eliminates inferential uncertainty about elite motivation, making it one of the framework''s clearest demonstrations of belief as infrastructure for extraction. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/sudans-islamic-turn-elite-appropriation-of-belief-and-the-fracturing-of-a-nation-1956-2011', 'https://archive.org/details/sudans-islamic-turn-elite-appropriation-of-belief-and-the-fracturing-of-a-nation-1956-2011', 'internet-archive:sudans-islamic-turn-elite-appropriation-of-belief-and-the-fracturing-of-a-nation-1956-2011'),
