  ('1090293276346617381', 'Switzerland, From Mercenary Confederation To Federal State ( 1291 1848)', 'Thomas J, Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This comprehensive study examines elite power mechanisms in Switzerland across 734 years (1291-2025), tracing how oligarchic families maintained concentrated wealth and control through multiple regime transformations—from mercenary confederation to democratic federal state to global financial center. The analysis demonstrates how Swiss elites successfully adapted from medieval mercenary contractors to industrial capitalists to banking magnates while preserving approximately 40-50% wealth concentration in the top 1% across all periods, despite universal suffrage (1848 men, 1971 women), comprehensive welfare state construction, and democratic institutions. Key mechanisms analyzed include mercenary capitalism and violence export (1291-1800s), banking secrecy institutionalization (1934), strategic elite concessions through "social partnership" labor relations (1937-present), manipulation of direct democracy through campaign financing, and coordination across language/religious divisions that fragmented workers. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/switzerland-from-mercenary-confederation-to-federal-state-1291-1848', 'https://archive.org/details/switzerland-from-mercenary-confederation-to-federal-state-1291-1848', 'internet-archive:switzerland-from-mercenary-confederation-to-federal-state-1291-1848'),
