  ('166338282974826916', 'Syria, From French Mandate To Catastrophe How Colonial Sectarian Engineering Led To Civil War ( 1920 2024)', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This case study examines how French mandatory rule in Syria (1920-1946) deliberately engineered sectarian divisions through discriminatory military recruitment, creating an Alawite-dominated armed forces that would rule Syria as a minoritarian dictatorship for 75 years after independence. It traces how France''s policy of recruiting Alawites disproportionately into the Troupes Spéciales—justified by racist "warlike races" theory—established power structures that led directly to the Assad dynasty (1970-2024) and the Syrian Civil War (2011-2024) that killed over 500,000 people. The study demonstrates how colonial institutional engineering determines post-colonial trajectories across generations, with French choices made in the 1920s-1940s producing catastrophic sectarian violence 65+ years after formal independence. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/syria-from-french-mandate-to-catastrophe-how-colonial-sectarian-engineering-led-', 'https://archive.org/details/syria-from-french-mandate-to-catastrophe-how-colonial-sectarian-engineering-led-', 'internet-archive:syria-from-french-mandate-to-catastrophe-how-colonial-sectarian-engineering-led-'),
