  ('667475206980403918', 'terra preta pilot v5', 'Kase Branham', 2026, 'Science', 'Tropical ferralsols cover approximately one billion hectares globally and support roughly half of all terrestrial agriculture. These soils fix 70–90% of applied phosphate fertilizer on iron and aluminum oxide surfaces, reducing P use efficiency to below 25% and forcing repeated fertilizer application from a non-renewable phosphate rock supply. Reanalysis of 3,689 geochemical samples from Amazonian Dark Earth (ADE) reveals that terra preta achieves 37% phosphorus retention efficiency versus 1% in adjacent ferralsol —a 37× difference—by transforming the iron oxides that normally fix phosphorus into ferrimagnetic phases that no longer immobilize it. This protocol describes a controlled incubation experiment to test whether the terra preta reactor mechanism can be replicated using three co-applied amendments: biochar (scaffold), agricultural lime (pH intervention), and an iron-reducing microbial inoculant (biological catalyst). The primary endpoint is a measurable increase in magnetic susceptibility coupled with a decrease in exchangeable iron and an increase in desorbable phosphorus—the three-way signature of the reactor. The experiment requires no specialized equipment beyond a portable magnetic susceptibility meter and standard soil chemistry, is designed for completion in 6–12 months, and costs approximately $18,000–$45,000 USD depending on analytical scope (a minimum-viable version measuring only MS, P, pH, and Fe at two timepoints is feasible for under $3,000). All materials, methods, and analysis code are provided as open-access resources. If successful, the protocol establishes a pathway toward an engineered soil amendment that simultaneously enhances food production, reduces fertilizer dependence, and sequesters carbon in stable mineral-organic complexes.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/terra_preta_pilot_v5', 'https://archive.org/details/terra_preta_pilot_v5', 'internet-archive:terra_preta_pilot_v5'),
  ('20102279344460840', 'Terre des hommes', 'Antoine de Saint-Exupéry', 1936, 'Biography', 'Terre des hommes by Antoine de Saint-Exupéry. A biography book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL86701W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL86701W-'),
  ('775687531583979433', 'Terre des hommes', 'Antoine de Saint-Exupéry', 1936, 'Biography', 'Terre des hommes by Antoine de Saint-Exupéry', 'https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/967072-L.jpg', 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL86701W', 'internet-archive:ol-works-OL86701W'),
  ('251214436535166461', 'Terres incertaines', 'Sergio Dalla Bernardina', 2014, 'Science', 'Terres incertaines by Sergio Dalla Bernardina. A science book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL31640626W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL31640626W-'),
  ('147446230659217168', 'Terrestrial Biosphere and Global Change', 'Brian Walker, Will Steffen, John Ingram', 1999, 'Science', 'Terrestrial Biosphere and Global Change by Brian Walker, Will Steffen, John Ingram. A science book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL21080806W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL21080806W-'),
  ('585169489754815917', 'Territories: A Map of What the Map Can''t Hold', 'Anonymous', 2026, 'Literature', 'Territories: A Map of What the Map Can''t Hold An annotated reading guide spanning philosophy, mathematics, literature, ecology, critical theory, and contemplative traditions — organized not as a list but as a landscape. The book is split into two halves. Unmapped is the analytical edge: formal systems that break against their own limits, consciousness trying to catch itself in the act, signs and power, living systems that organize without a blueprint, and the void where pattern thins out. Unfathomable is the interior: grief that refuses to be past tense, desire that won''t fit the available forms, the space between people, and joy so fierce it frightens you. Each half contains five territories organized around a question. Entries are annotated with editorial asides that think out loud rather than argue, and connected by a cross-reference system of Folds and Ruptures that compress distance or expose what a framework costs. Density markers (○ ◐ ●) indicate weight — accessible, sustained, or demanding — without implying quality. The structure is non-sequential. Enter anywhere. The architecture holds from any angle because the connections between subjects are real and multiple and not owned by any single path through them. Published anonymously. Released under CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/Territories', 'https://archive.org/details/Territories', 'internet-archive:Territories'),
  ('463345166760682663', 'Territorios de la incertidumbre', 'Omar Tobío', 2012, 'Science', 'Territorios de la incertidumbre by Omar Tobío. A science book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL22346981W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL22346981W-'),
