  ('1031746168573724453', 'THE AMERICAN MARIUS, How The End Of The Draft Broke The Republic''s Emergency Brake', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This study compares the Roman Republic''s Marian military reforms of 107 BC with the United States'' transition to an all-volunteer military force in 1973, analyzing how both structural changes severed the feedback loop between citizen-voters and the human cost of war. It examines how professional military establishments economically dependent on continuous operations—Roman legionaries seeking land grants and American defense contractors seeking perpetual contracts—create political pressure for permanent mobilization that democratic institutions cannot effectively constrain, tracing Rome''s 80-year trajectory from reform to empire and assessing where the United States falls on a comparable timeline. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-american-marius-how-the-end-of-the-draft-broke-the-republics-emergency-brake', 'https://archive.org/details/the-american-marius-how-the-end-of-the-draft-broke-the-republics-emergency-brake', 'internet-archive:the-american-marius-how-the-end-of-the-draft-broke-the-republics-emergency-brake'),
