  ('1067486428819648030', 'The Animal Book : A Visual Encyclopedia of Life on Earth (DK Smithsonian)', 'Burnie, David, author', 2013, 'Literature', 'The Animal Book : A Visual Encyclopedia of Life on Earth (DK Smithsonian) Publisher : New York, New York : DK Publishing Dorling Kindersley Associated-names : Smithsonian Institution; DK Publishing, Inc ISBN-10 : 9781465414571 ISBN-13 : 1465414576 by : David Burnie (Author), Smithsonian Institution (Contributor) Publisher ‏ : ‎ DK Children; Illustrated edition (August 19, 2013) Language ‏ : ‎ English Hardcover ‏ : ‎ 304 pages Reading age ‏ : ‎ 3 - 8 years, from customers Grade level ‏ : ‎ 4 - 7 Veterinary Encyclopedias , Encyclopedias for Children , Children''s Zoology Books (Books) ISBN-10 ‏ : ‎ 1465414576 ISBN-13 ‏ : ‎ 978-1465414571 "Our world is filled with extraordinary diversity, from amoebas to zebras, from tiny toadstools to giant oaks. The wonders of the natural world are on display in The Animal Book. This guide to life on our planet is packed full of information about creatures big and small. This tome is structured according to scientific classification, with straightforward explanations of more than 1,500 specimens, each stunningly photographed. A "tree of life" greets readers at the beginning of the book, charting the complex and interconnected relationships between species. Every plant and animal is presented in proportion, with in-depth spreads giving a sense of scale to each organism. Feature spreads that focus on a single specimen let children get up close and personal with the world''s most fascinating animals, making The Animal Book perfect not only for homework help but to satisfy kids'' curiosity about the wealth of living creatures that inhabit our world"--Amazon.com, viewed August 22, 2013 At head fo title: DK Smithsonian This inspiring children’s reference guide welcomes you to the animal kingdom where you can meet more than 1,500 species, ranging from ants to zebras and everything in between. Stunning pictures bring you face to face with giant predators you know and love, including polar bears and tigers, as well as mysterious microscopic life, including amoebas and bacteria. A variety of animal habitats are shown in beautiful detail, while accessible information, additional fact boxes, and amazing galleries complete the stories. A jaw-dropping spectrum of animal types – from fish and birds to reptiles and mammals – provides a learning experience like no other.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-animal-book_202403', 'https://archive.org/details/the-animal-book_202403', 'internet-archive:the-animal-book_202403'),
  ('787837091088506814', 'The Animal-Lover''s Book of Beastly Murder', 'Patricia Highsmith', 1975, 'Crime', 'The Animal-Lover''s Book of Beastly Murder by Patricia Highsmith. A crime book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL59443W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL59443W-'),
  ('690330685480237196', 'The Animal-Lover''s Book of Beastly Murder', 'Patricia Highsmith', 1975, 'Crime', 'The Animal-Lover''s Book of Beastly Murder by Patricia Highsmith', 'https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/250425-L.jpg', 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL59443W', 'internet-archive:ol-works-OL59443W'),
  ('947017653903062857', 'The Ankara Catastrophe, Institutional Power Vs Personal Charisma', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This case study examines why the Ottoman Empire survived catastrophic military defeat at Ankara in 1402 and reached its zenith under Süleyman the Magnificent (1520-1566), while Timur''s empire fragmented within three years of his death in 1405 despite his unmatched military genius. It demonstrates how institutional development—particularly the timar system, devshirme recruitment, janissary corps, and integration of the ulema into governance—created force multipliers that allowed competent Ottoman rulers to achieve what even brilliant rulers like Timur could not accomplish through personal charisma alone. The study analyzes the propaganda battle between Bayezid and Timur, the Battle of Ankara, the Ottoman Interregnum (1402-1413), and the divergent trajectories that followed, revealing how institutionalized belief systems determine elite power durability across generations. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-ankara-catastrophe-institutional-power-vs-personal-charisma', 'https://archive.org/details/the-ankara-catastrophe-institutional-power-vs-personal-charisma', 'internet-archive:the-ankara-catastrophe-institutional-power-vs-personal-charisma'),
