  ('1121796914453408751', 'The Authoritarian Dynamic', 'Karen Stenner', 2001, 'Politics', 'The Authoritarian Dynamic by Karen Stenner. A politics book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL8324140W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL8324140W-'),
  ('367736794315246154', 'The Authority Capture Mechanism', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This paper identifies and documents the Authority Capture Mechanism — the recurring pattern across civilizations in which strategic actors deliberately seek positions of accumulated symbolic authority not to serve their original function but to exploit the verification override that generations of genuine service have built into the office. Drawing on cases spanning three millennia and five civilizations with no contact with one another — the Borgia papacy, Cardinal Richelieu and Cardinal Wolsey in Christendom, the Umayyad seizure of the Islamic caliphate, the High Priests of Amun in pharaonic Egypt, Japanese Buddhist warrior monks, and Chinese palace eunuchs — it demonstrates through convergent evolution that institutional trust is a resource that attracts capture, and that the better an institution serves its original function, the more vulnerable it becomes to actors who recognize that accumulated trust as an exploitable asset. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-authority-capture-mechanism', 'https://archive.org/details/the-authority-capture-mechanism', 'internet-archive:the-authority-capture-mechanism'),
