  ('412322033773627904', 'The Cage Rebuilt', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This theoretical essay analyzes three major revolutions—France (1789), Haiti (1791), and Russia (1917)—to demonstrate why revolutionary movements systematically reproduce the hierarchical structures they destroy. Through comparative case analysis, it argues that the pattern is not a failure of revolutionary will but a structural inevitability: advanced civilizations require hierarchical coordination to sustain large populations, and these coordination systems are inherently vulnerable to capture by cynical operators regardless of revolutionary ideology. The study examines the "metabolic trap" that forces post-revolutionary societies to rebuild authority structures, and why these structures attract the same types of elite operators that controlled pre-revolutionary hierarchies. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-cage-rebuilt', 'https://archive.org/details/the-cage-rebuilt', 'internet-archive:the-cage-rebuilt'),
  ('1149019340041124066', 'The Caine mutiny', 'Herman Wouk', 1951, 'Drama', 'The Caine mutiny by Herman Wouk. A drama book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL729088W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL729088W-'),
