  ('520868505523845326', 'The Cat Was Grue: Goodman’s Riddle, Schrödinger’s Cat, and the Grammar of Quantum Reality', 'Sum Bunny', 2026, 'Science', 'Schrödinger’s cat is usually framed as a paradox of physics: how a system can be both alive and dead until observed. This essay argues that the deeper paradox is linguistic. Drawing on Nelson Goodman’s “grue” paradox, modern decoherence theory, and the mathematics of projective geometry, the paper proposes that quantum measurement is best understood as the ecological selection of stable descriptive grammars rather than the physical destruction of a wavefunction. Multiple incompatible but valid predicates can describe the same underlying quantum state; interaction with the environment filters these possibilities, favoring those that are dynamically stable under local coupling. In this view, “collapse” is not a mysterious physical event but a representational bottleneck: the enforced convergence of many semantic trajectories into a single classical description, much as parallel lines converge to a vanishing point in perspective drawing. The essay preserves unitarity, wavefunction realism, and the standard account of decoherence, while cleanly separating the structure of measurement outcomes from the unresolved problem of quantum probabilities. Published by Ark Hive Press as part of the Sum Bunny canon. Licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 (CC BY 4.0).', 'https://archive.org/services/img/cat-was-grue-sum-bunny-2026', 'https://archive.org/details/cat-was-grue-sum-bunny-2026', 'internet-archive:cat-was-grue-sum-bunny-2026'),
