  ('234835835617036981', 'The Complete German Literature Reading List ( 238 Works)', 'Maria', 2026, 'Literature', 'This list was created to accompany my YouTube video “German Literature: The Complete Reading List” on my channel Strange Lucidity. I drew inspiration from dozens of sources — especially major books on German and world literature, university reading lists (where available), and material from my own literature courses. I cross-referenced all of this to compile a list of 238 works that, in my view, form the backbone of German literature across the centuries. I list my main soucres in the description box of the video. The goal of this list — and of the video — is to give you a map that helps you navigate German literature and choose your own reading path. Feel free to print it, annotate it, cross things out — whatever helps you engage with it. In this PDF, I have underlined the works that I personally consider the most influential, most widely read, and most worth your time if you want to engage seriously with German literature. These are the books that, in my estimation, have had the most far-reaching influence both inside and outside the German language. This list focuses on primary literature — mostly fiction. Works of philosophy, sociology, psychology, or literary theory are not included, because otherwise the scope would become unmanageable. Of course, reading thinkers such as Kant, Fichte, Freud, Hegel, Marx, Adorno, and many others is an essential second step for anyone seriously engaging with the German intellectual tradition. I may compile such a list in the future, but it is not part of this one. Another thing not included is recommendations for specific translations. I mentioned titles whenever possible in English – as this list is aimed to help readers who don’t speak German – this does not mean, however, that I endorse that particular edition or translation. That will be part of a later research project. If you notice a mistake or think I’ve overlooked an important author or work, feel free to leave a comment below the video or email me at strange.lucidity0@gmail.com. I’m happy to revise the PDF based on thoughtful suggestions, and I’ll pin a comment under the video to make any changes transparent. All used images are in the public domain. If you’d like to support my work, consider checking out my literature courses on teachable or leaving me a donation on my kofi-account. Both are always linked below my videos. I hope your reading of German Literature brings you much joy and insight! All the best, Maria', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-complete-german-literature-reading-list-238-works', 'https://archive.org/details/the-complete-german-literature-reading-list-238-works', 'internet-archive:the-complete-german-literature-reading-list-238-works'),
