  ('185823486972923001', 'The Congo Deception, How A Christian Monarch Exploited Information Asymmetry And The Verification Override To Commit One Of History’s Greatest Atrocities', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This case study examines King Leopold II''s Congo Free State (1876-1908) as a demonstration of two complementary theoretical frameworks: the Information Asymmetry Theory of Scaled Domination and the Verification Override — the mechanism by which symbolic authority selectively disables a population''s capacity for critical evaluation of elite claims. Using Leopold''s private correspondence, institutional maneuvering, and the Belgian public''s decades-long failure to recognize documented atrocities, it shows how a Christian monarch engineered and maintained information control over a system that killed an estimated ten million Congolese, and how that control collapsed not through better evidence but through structural disruption of the information architecture by Edmund Dene Morel and Roger Casement. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-congo-deception-how-a-christian-monarch-exploited-information-asymmetry-and-', 'https://archive.org/details/the-congo-deception-how-a-christian-monarch-exploited-information-asymmetry-and-', 'internet-archive:the-congo-deception-how-a-christian-monarch-exploited-information-asymmetry-and-'),
  ('170084150152465474', 'The Congress of Vienna', 'Harold Nicolson', 1946, 'Politics', 'The Congress of Vienna by Harold Nicolson. A politics book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL10425175W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL10425175W-'),
  ('1086734880776772090', 'The Conquered Conquerors, Elite Power, The Conversion Economy, And The Transformation Of Iranian Civilization, 224– 1010 CE', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'Historical Fiction', 'This comprehensive case study examines the Islamization of Persia from the Arab conquest of the Sassanid Empire (651 CE) through the completion of conversion and the Persian-Islamic synthesis by 1010 CE, analyzing how material incentives rather than spiritual conviction drove three centuries of gradual conversion through a "conversion economy" that made Islam progressively more rational to adopt at every income level and social position. It traces the structural vulnerabilities of the Sassanid belief-power system, the mechanics of early Islamic governance under the dhimma framework that created fiscal and social pressures on the Zoroastrian population, and how the Umayyad caliphate''s contradiction between universalist Islamic theology and Arab ethnic privilege generated the specific grievances that powered the Abbasid Revolution. The study demonstrates how the Persian elite, beginning with the Abbasid Revolution of 750 CE, appropriated Islam to seize the caliphate from the Arab conquerors and progressively transformed Islamic civilization into a Persian-dominated culture, culminating in the Shahnameh of Ferdowsi (c. 1010)—a work that celebrates pre-Islamic Persian civilization while affirming Islamic faith, embodying the complete synthesis of two civilizations and the internalization of Islamic identity deep enough to make pre-Islamic Persian culture safe to celebrate rather than threatening to suppress. The study demonstrates how the conquered eventually conquered their conquerors through theological and administrative appropriation, creating the Persian-Islamic synthesis that would dominate medieval Islamic civilization. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-conquered-conquerors-elite-power-the-conversion-economy-and-the-transformati', 'https://archive.org/details/the-conquered-conquerors-elite-power-the-conversion-economy-and-the-transformati', 'internet-archive:the-conquered-conquerors-elite-power-the-conversion-economy-and-the-transformati'),
