  ('578060141749398453', 'The Constant, A Theory Of Elite Power, Belief, And The Architecture Of Compliance', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This paper presents the capstone theoretical statement of the Elite Power Dynamics (EPD) analytical framework, examining the universal structural dynamic through which small governing elites maintain control over large governed populations across every civilization type in the historical record, from ancient Mesopotamia through contemporary liberal democracies. It analyzes the two core instruments of governing power — organized force and organized belief — detailing how belief functions to naturalize hierarchy, pre-position dehumanization, and produce self-policing populations that enforce elite arrangements without instruction or awareness. The paper also outlines the sequential stages through which governing systems convert coerced populations into genuine believers, and identifies the structural conditions under which every governing arrangement eventually becomes vulnerable to challenge. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-constant-a-theory-of-elite-power-belief-and-the-architecture-of-compliance', 'https://archive.org/details/the-constant-a-theory-of-elite-power-belief-and-the-architecture-of-compliance', 'internet-archive:the-constant-a-theory-of-elite-power-belief-and-the-architecture-of-compliance'),
  ('334824608695427475', 'The Constraint Law of Generativity: A Minimal Constraint Framework Across Physical, Biological, Cognitive, and Social Systems', 'Sum Bunny', 2026, 'Science', 'The Constraint Law of Generativity presents a minimal cross-domain framework for understanding how systems balance change and persistence. Framed as the inequality D ≤ R, the essay proposes that no system can generate novelty faster than it can absorb failure without undergoing nonlinear reorganization or loss of structure. Rather than introducing a new physical law, this work defines a reusable constraint template that appears across multiple domains, including physics, biology, cognition, and social systems. It connects phenomena such as shock formation, phase transitions, instability-driven restructuring, cognitive overload, and institutional breakdown as structurally analogous responses to limits in coordination, information flow, and energy dissipation. The framework emphasizes boundary dependence, scale effects, and the necessity of independently estimating system capacity. It distinguishes clearly between structural explanation and moral justification, preventing misuse while maintaining analytical rigor. Positioned within the Sum Bunny canon (Ark Hive Press), this essay functions as a boundary condition on generative systems, complementing earlier work that defines their internal dynamics. Its contribution lies in compression and portability: offering a concise way to identify when systems adapt, reorganize, or fail.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/constraint-law-of-generativity-sum-bunny-2026', 'https://archive.org/details/constraint-law-of-generativity-sum-bunny-2026', 'internet-archive:constraint-law-of-generativity-sum-bunny-2026'),
  ('331969111427137506', 'The construction of logical space', 'Agustín Rayo', 2013, 'Science', 'The construction of logical space by Agustín Rayo. A science book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL23216926W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL23216926W-'),
  ('748410840587436855', 'The Contender', 'Robert Lipsyte', 1967, 'Sports', 'The Contender by Robert Lipsyte. A sports book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL2704583W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL2704583W-'),
  ('375768577589218698', 'The Contender', 'Robert Lipsyte', 1967, 'Sports', 'The Contender by Robert Lipsyte', 'https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/4939721-L.jpg', 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL2704583W', 'internet-archive:ol-works-OL2704583W'),
  ('989622624690311649', 'The contracting universe', 'Rupert Raynar', 2009, 'Science', 'The contracting universe by Rupert Raynar. A science book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL32238164W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL32238164W-'),
  ('311971260800435042', 'The Conversation Between Kings', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This theoretical essay extends the Patterns of Power framework from its internal analysis of single civilizations to the inter-civilizational layer, arguing that when two advanced civilizations meet, their conscious elites recognize each other as structurally identical — each the apex of a belief-based extraction hierarchy — and that this mutual recognition produces a distinct operational communication layer, visible in sources like the Amarna letters and the Cyrus Cylinder, in which the theological language of official records gives way to entirely material negotiation over military capacity, tribute, and administrative arrangements. The essay develops a typology of four conquest strategies available to militarily superior powers — pure extraction (the Mongol method), elite replacement (the Spanish conquest of Mesoamerica), parallel systems (the Achaemenid Persian empire), and appropriation with synthesis (Alexander, the Ptolemies, Rome''s interpretatio romana, and the Islamic conquests) — demonstrating through comparative analysis that the material logic of cheap governance consistently favors the fourth strategy, explaining why most major civilizational transitions produce hybrid belief systems and retained administrative classes rather than wholesale replacement. It further identifies the "mirror problem" — the destabilizing effect on a conquered population''s belief system when visible foreign rule contradicts divine order claims — and the "retained elite" dynamic, in which conquered administrative classes are preserved not from sentiment but because they hold the tacit operational knowledge required to run complex extraction systems. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-conversation-between-kings', 'https://archive.org/details/the-conversation-between-kings', 'internet-archive:the-conversation-between-kings'),
  ('881606088753320203', 'The Cook''s Encyclopaedia', 'Tom Stobart', 1980, 'Cooking', 'The Cook''s Encyclopaedia by Tom Stobart. A cooking book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL6092334W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL6092334W-'),
