  ('925785793560496403', 'The Dhamma Paradox, Imperial Belief Management And Its Consequences In Ashoka''s Mauryan Empire, C. 268– 185 BCE', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'Historical Fiction', 'This case study examines Ashoka''s dhamma program in the Mauryan Empire (c. 268–185 BCE), analyzing how a sincere Buddhist king constructed an imperial belief system that systematically excluded Buddhist doctrinal content in favor of a deliberately vague, universalist moral framework designed to govern religious diversity across an empire of continental scale. It traces how Ashoka''s renunciation of military conquest after the Kalinga campaign created the political space for the dhamma innovation—a governance framework explicitly designed to avoid triggering elite conflict with the Brahminical establishment whose institutional authority and economic interests the program most directly challenged through the withdrawal of royal yajna patronage and the reorientation of resources toward Buddhist institutions and state welfare infrastructure. The study demonstrates how the dhamma program operated as invention-based rather than appropriation-based belief governance, creating institutional infrastructure (the dhamma mahamattas, welfare apparatus, rock edicts) entirely dependent on imperial patronage for operation, with no independent resource base, no autonomous interpretive authority, and no mechanism for institutional survival beyond the emperor''s personal will. It analyzes how the complete collapse of the dhamma system within a generation of Ashoka''s death—triggered by succession crisis and the senapati''s (military commander''s) coup that installed the Brahminical Shunga dynasty—confirms the framework''s prediction that patronage-dependent systems lack the institutional resilience to survive military succession. Most significantly, it reveals the paradox that the belief system''s failure in its political homeland was the condition for its global success: the Buddhist missionary communities that Ashoka sent beyond Indian state power were forced to develop independent institutional roots and community formation processes that proved far more durable than the state-dependent dhamma program, resulting in a tradition that survives today across Asia in forms descended from those diaspora communities rather than from the Indian imperial synthesis. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-dhamma-paradox-imperial-belief-management-and-its-consequences-in-ashokas-ma', 'https://archive.org/details/the-dhamma-paradox-imperial-belief-management-and-its-consequences-in-ashokas-ma', 'internet-archive:the-dhamma-paradox-imperial-belief-management-and-its-consequences-in-ashokas-ma'),
