  ('841159568996452994', 'The Ebionites, Christianity''s Suppressed Alternative And The Imperial Capture Of A Social Revolution', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'Historical Fiction', 'This study examines the Ebionites, an early Jewish-Christian sect (1st-5th centuries CE) that maintained Jesus''s original emphasis on economic redistribution, voluntary poverty, and resistance to Temple-based extraction systems. It analyzes how the Ebionite movement was systematically suppressed as Christianity underwent Romanization under Paul''s influence and Constantine''s imperial patronage, documenting how their texts were destroyed and their communal ownership practices were condemned as heretical. The near-total erasure of Ebionite literature demonstrates the thoroughness with which alternative Christian voices were silenced once orthodoxy allied with state power. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-ebionites-christianitys-suppressed-alternative-and-the-imperial-capture-of-a-social-revolution', 'https://archive.org/details/the-ebionites-christianitys-suppressed-alternative-and-the-imperial-capture-of-a-social-revolution', 'internet-archive:the-ebionites-christianitys-suppressed-alternative-and-the-imperial-capture-of-a-social-revolution'),
