  ('784487497749380711', 'The Essential Leonard Read', 'Leonard E. Read', 2016, 'non-fiction', 'Leonard Read (1898-1983), founder of the Foundation for Economic Education, dedicated his life to spreading “the freedom philosophy.” In addition to facilitating the contributions of others, Read himself was a prolific author and a font of wisdom. For example, in the included "How Socialism Harms the Individual," Read explains how the forced transfer of wealth degrades and cripples everyone involved. In addition to being directly harmed, the victim of the transfer becomes less provident and charitable. The beneficiary becomes less self-reliant and capable. And the enforcer of the transfer becomes power-addled and contemptible. Power corrupts and captivity degrades. Read cuts through the weeds of public policy debate, and counsels the reader to renounce any role in the redistribution of wealth "for his own mental and spiritual health." In “I, Pencil,” his most famous work (also included), Read charmingly adopts the voice of a pencil who recounts the tale of its own ancestry. Despite its humble appearance, the pencil is the end result of a mind-boggling, globe-spanning feat of cooperation among millions of strangers. This triumph of coordination is all the more marvelous for having no mastermind. Indeed no central planner, however brilliant and public-minded, could have pulled it off. Such an intricate order can only emerge out of the voluntary interplay of free people. Read imparts to the reader his own sense of wonder at the miracles of the market. After reading Read, you’ll never look at the abundance that surrounds you the same again.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/TheEssentialLeonardRead', 'https://archive.org/details/TheEssentialLeonardRead', 'internet-archive:TheEssentialLeonardRead'),
  ('556481553574987157', 'The Essential Turing', 'Alan Mathison Turing, Jack Copeland', 2002, 'Technology', 'The Essential Turing by Alan Mathison Turing, Jack Copeland. A technology book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL8010409W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL8010409W-'),
  ('981499759066167724', 'The essentials of Buddhist philosophy', 'Junjirō Takakusu, Charles A. Moore, Chan Wing Tsit', 1935, 'Philosophy', 'The essentials of Buddhist philosophy by Junjirō Takakusu, Charles A. Moore, Chan Wing Tsit. A philosophy book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL4542996W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL4542996W-'),
  ('107213806470571314', 'The essentials of Buddhist philosophy', 'Junjirō Takakusu, Charles A. Moore, Chan Wing Tsit', 1935, 'Philosophy', 'The essentials of Buddhist philosophy by Junjirō Takakusu, Charles A. Moore, Chan Wing Tsit', 'https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/8516963-L.jpg', 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL4542996W', 'internet-archive:ol-works-OL4542996W'),
  ('1017911029751999189', 'The Eternal Flame - Defending the Sacred Soul of Sanatan Dharma Through a Millenium of Fire', 'Pranav Singh Visen', 2026, 'History', 'The Eternal Flame by Pranav Singh Visen is a powerful civilizational manifesto documenting Bharat’s 1,000-year struggle for survival. Moving beyond "sanitized" history, it examines the trauma of physical genocide, temple desecration, and the systematic destruction of ancient universities like Nalanda. The book celebrates the "Lions of Dharma"—the Rajputs, Marathas, and Sikhs—whose relentless resistance preserved India’s spiritual soul. It also addresses modern intellectual and legal challenges, urging a national reawakening. Ultimately, it is a call to action for a new generation of "Intellectual Kshatriyas" to reclaim their heritage and restore Bharat as a global guide (Vishwa Guru).', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-eternal-flame-sanatanam', 'https://archive.org/details/the-eternal-flame-sanatanam', 'internet-archive:the-eternal-flame-sanatanam'),
