  ('1032502445014004576', 'The Execution Of Socrates And The Defense Of Epistemic Monopoly', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'Classic Literature', 'This study examines the trial and execution of Socrates in 399 BCE as a case of information monopoly defense operating through democratic legal mechanisms. It analyzes how Socrates'' dialectical method (the elenchus) systematically exposed that Athens'' paid teachers (the Sophists) and political leaders could not define the very virtues they claimed to possess, threatening a lucrative educational industry worth thousands of drachmae and undermining the epistemological foundations of elite political authority. The case demonstrates how democratic Athens coordinated the elimination of this threat through public delegitimization (Aristophanes'' The Clouds , 423 BCE), political opportunity (aftermath of the Peloponnesian War and the tyranny of Socrates'' student Critias), and formal charges framing epistemological threat as religious crime—impiety and corrupting the youth. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-execution-of-socrates-and-the-defense-of-epistemic-monopoly', 'https://archive.org/details/the-execution-of-socrates-and-the-defense-of-epistemic-monopoly', 'internet-archive:the-execution-of-socrates-and-the-defense-of-epistemic-monopoly'),
  ('968986573665568676', 'The executioner''s men', 'George W. Grayson, Samuel Logan, George W. Grayson', 2012, 'True Crime', 'The executioner''s men by George W. Grayson, Samuel Logan, George W. Grayson. A true crime book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL16118127W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL16118127W-'),
