  ('255556292728903201', 'The Exploit, Human Cognitive Architecture And The Permanent Vulnerability To Elite Capture', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This study examines the cognitive architecture underlying elite power across civilizations, arguing that human psychological features — including the authority heuristic, coalition loyalty instinct, sacred value lock, narrative capture, and sunk cost of formation — constitute a permanent "exploit" that governing classes have learned to activate through millennia of trial and error. It analyzes how the mismatch between the Pleistocene-evolved mind and large-scale anonymous civilizational environments creates structural vulnerabilities that formation systems systematically target, and argues that institutional friction — rather than individual recognition — is the only available constraint on this exploitation. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-exploit-human-cognitive-architecture-and-the-permanent-vulnerability-to-elite-capture', 'https://archive.org/details/the-exploit-human-cognitive-architecture-and-the-permanent-vulnerability-to-elite-capture', 'internet-archive:the-exploit-human-cognitive-architecture-and-the-permanent-vulnerability-to-elite-capture'),
