  ('1098149769178139321', 'The French Revolution, Dual Infrastructure And The Battle For France', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This case study examines the French Revolution (1789-1815) as Western civilization''s most explicit battle between its dual belief infrastructures—Judeo-Christian religious legitimation versus Greco-Roman secular republicanism. It analyzes how revolutionary elites appropriated classical natural rights philosophy and republican virtue while counter-revolutionary elites deployed divine right monarchy and Catholic hierarchy to advance competing extraction systems, documenting church property confiscation, feudalism''s abolition, the Terror''s violence, and the Vendée genocide. The study demonstrates how neither belief system could be eliminated despite unprecedented suppression attempts, forcing synthesis solutions (Napoleon''s Concordat, Charter of 1814) that reflected Western dual infrastructure constraints, and includes comparative analysis with Islamic and Chinese single-infrastructure civilizations to illuminate patterns unique to Western political development. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-french-revolution-dual-infrastructure-and-the-battle-for-france', 'https://archive.org/details/the-french-revolution-dual-infrastructure-and-the-battle-for-france', 'internet-archive:the-french-revolution-dual-infrastructure-and-the-battle-for-france'),
