  ('671194225188596918', 'The Future Traditions Foundation — 2019: A Record of the Final Year', '''Iolani Pu''u Kolenc (formerly known as Misa Miele Mandigo Kelly, born Annette Marie Puu)', 2026, 'History', 'Description: An archival record and act of unerasing. Documents the final year of the Future Traditions Foundation — a California nonprofit arts organization supporting contemporary dance, fiscal sponsorship, and international collaboration, founded in 1998 and led through 2019 by ''Iolani Pu''u Kolenc. Assembled from contemporaneous correspondence, organizational records, and personal communications spanning January through November 2019, this archive establishes the founding lineage of SonneBlauma Danscz Theatre → ArtBark International → Future Traditions Foundation, and preserves the administrative, financial, and creative contributions of founder and Executive Director ''Iolani Pu''u Kolenc across two decades of California contemporary dance history. Contains correspondence with international collaborators in Slovenia, Turkey, and New York; documentation of fiscal sponsorship activities; board records; and a first-person account of organizational events of 2019. Assembled and annotated by the founder in February 2026 as part of a decolonization harm healing practice. After working on the archival records for 1998, 1999, 2000, I chose to fast forward to the final year, and rip the bandage off so to speak, expose the infection, use a factual archive to help confirm my understanding of what went down. I was beginning to see, as I reviewed earlier years, that the "takeover" and "annihilation" of what was my life work, it was a process, and it was a slow takeover of the Foundation. In my current evolutionary phase, I realized, you just can''t move on and bury one''s personal truth of what happened. In order to evolve, you have to have the courage to sit with the journey, and witness what was there, and feel the complexity of all the emotions. It is as if, I have been healing from the harmful/shadow aspects of the Western ways of producing/collaborating and interfacing with people with "dark traits" knowing such people exist, but choosing, to survive, to focus on the beauty and positive in life, and pretend the rest didn''t exist. It is also medicine for the impact of gaslighting, and what the takeaway was, is that an individual took advantage of a condition I had, knowing what it is like to have a dissociative black out, and filled the narrative in as his/her truth, which was counter than my own recollection. I did not begin to process all of it, until I left the relationship, which is actually, "normal" given the circumstances. Stepping out of the relationship was the gift of decolonizing relationship, and owning, that contemporary dance, has a very dark shadow indeed. I have no desire in this present moment to return into the field of the arts and am not sure yet what is emerging, but taking the time to unerase my lifework, and publish it into the future, seems a necessary bit of tidying up. What this archive demonstrates is that the record was always there. The emails, the budgets, the international correspondence, the copyright, the creative vision — all of it intact, waiting to be witnessed. The founder witnessed it. And in witnessing it, named what was true: that the work was hers, that the taking was real, and that surviving it — and stepping out of it — required a courage that is now documented alongside the work itself. This archive is both the evidence and the act of healing.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/ftf-2019-archive', 'https://archive.org/details/ftf-2019-archive', 'internet-archive:ftf-2019-archive'),
  ('178430704547161259', 'The Gabriel Hounds', 'Mary Stewart', 1967, 'Mystery', 'The Gabriel Hounds by Mary Stewart. A mystery book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL213268W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL213268W-'),
