  ('1105068895587854127', 'The God King''s Mountain, Hydraulic Power, Divine Kingship, And The Infrastructure Of Angkor', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This case study examines elite control mechanisms in the Khmer Empire (802–1431 CE), analyzing how the devaraja (god-king) cult, temple-mountain architecture, and an extensive hydraulic irrigation network functioned as a unified system of cosmological legitimation and material extraction across the Cambodian plain. It demonstrates how the Angkorian state''s collapse resulted not from military conquest but from a structural contradiction between the theological mandate for continuous temple construction and the hydraulic maintenance demands of the irrigation infrastructure that underpinned the entire system''s agricultural surplus. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-god-kings-mountain-hydraulic-power-divine-kingship-and-the-infrastructure-of-angkor', 'https://archive.org/details/the-god-kings-mountain-hydraulic-power-divine-kingship-and-the-infrastructure-of-angkor', 'internet-archive:the-god-kings-mountain-hydraulic-power-divine-kingship-and-the-infrastructure-of-angkor'),
