  ('276815528328630531', 'The Great Redundancy, The Withering Away Of The Proletariat And The Coming Egalitarianism Of Elites', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This theoretical synthesis argues that technological development is eliminating the three dependencies (labor, military service, mass consumption) that historically gave the underclass structural leverage, pointing not toward Marx''s predicted proletarian revolution but toward proletarian obsolescence through gradual "structural liquidation." Drawing on historical precedents including the English Enclosures and Highland Clearances, it projects an endpoint of universal elite egalitarianism—the rights-based governance elites have always practiced among themselves—now extended to all surviving humanity because no subordinate class remains to dominate. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-great-redundancy-the-withering-away-of-the-proletariat-and-the-coming-egalitarianism-of-elites', 'https://archive.org/details/the-great-redundancy-the-withering-away-of-the-proletariat-and-the-coming-egalitarianism-of-elites', 'internet-archive:the-great-redundancy-the-withering-away-of-the-proletariat-and-the-coming-egalitarianism-of-elites'),
