  ('496608557686721190', 'The Mamluk Sultans And The Puppet Caliph, Appropriating Existing Belief Infrastructure', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This case study examines the Mamluk Sultanate (1250-1517 CE) in Egypt and Syria, analyzing how slave-soldier elites who seized power through military force maintained the Abbasid Caliphate as a powerless puppet institution in Cairo to provide Islamic legitimacy for their rule. For 267 years—despite averaging only 5-7 years per sultan and enduring constant internal power struggles—every Mamluk ruler sought caliphal investiture, demonstrating that belief infrastructure was a structural necessity rather than optional feature of governance. The case reveals elite appropriation of existing belief systems as a more efficient strategy than constructing new ideology, paralleling the Japanese Shogunate''s maintenance of the Emperor (1603-1868) and proving that military power alone cannot scale to govern millions without legitimating institutions. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-mamluk-sultans-and-the-puppet-caliph-appropriating-existing-belief-infrastructure', 'https://archive.org/details/the-mamluk-sultans-and-the-puppet-caliph-appropriating-existing-belief-infrastructure', 'internet-archive:the-mamluk-sultans-and-the-puppet-caliph-appropriating-existing-belief-infrastructure'),
