  ('966524670379150857', 'The Mechanics Of Proxy Trust', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This theoretical paper examines the universal mechanism by which proxy signals—credentials, awards, certifications, and institutional affiliations—emerge to solve genuine coordination problems, then produce verification overrides that exempt signal-holders from critical scrutiny, and finally attract capture by parties who seek control over the signal-granting process rather than the underlying quality it represents. Using the Academy Awards as a detailed illustration, the paper demonstrates how the same three-stage sequence (signal emergence, verification override, signal capture) operates across all domains where human societies must coordinate at scales exceeding their capacity for direct personal evaluation, including education, science, religion, medicine, and governance. The analysis draws on signaling theory, regulatory capture, bounded rationality, and cognitive psychology to show why this mechanism is structurally inevitable rather than the result of conspiracy or moral failure. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-mechanics-of-proxy-trust', 'https://archive.org/details/the-mechanics-of-proxy-trust', 'internet-archive:the-mechanics-of-proxy-trust'),
