  ('807792840779702512', 'The Pacific Confirmation, Hawaiian Kapu And The Independent Discovery Of Belief Infrastructure', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This study examines the Hawaiian kapu system and broader Polynesian religious frameworks, demonstrating that societies in complete geographic isolation (settled 400-600 CE, ~10,000 miles from Mesopotamia with zero contact until European arrival) independently developed identical legitimation mechanisms as Eurasian civilizations: divine genealogy (chiefs descended from creator gods Wakea-Papa), comprehensive sacred law systems (kapu/tapu), priestly classes (kahuna), death penalties for violations, and food prohibitions. The 1819 elite-initiated abolition without divine consequence proves the system''s political function, providing conclusive evidence that these narrative structures represent convergent evolution toward optimal governance solutions rather than cultural diffusion.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-pacific-confirmation-hawaiian-kapu-and-the-independent-discovery-of-belief-infrastructure', 'https://archive.org/details/the-pacific-confirmation-hawaiian-kapu-and-the-independent-discovery-of-belief-infrastructure', 'internet-archive:the-pacific-confirmation-hawaiian-kapu-and-the-independent-discovery-of-belief-infrastructure'),
