  ('98226447978745154', 'The Permanent Reformation, Elite Factions, Narrative Capture, And The Real Engine Of Western Political Change', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This paper analyzes Western political development — the Protestant Reformation, the Enlightenment, and the emergence of liberal democracy — through the lens of elite factional interest rather than intellectual or popular revolution, arguing that each transformation was driven by constrained elites appropriating available legitimating frameworks to justify institutional power they were already in the process of seizing. It traces this mechanism from Constantine''s fourth-century synthesis, which accidentally embedded two competing legitimating traditions into Western civilization, through Henry VIII''s break with Rome, the English Civil War, the Glorious Revolution, and the construction of liberal democratic ideology, with particular attention to what was systematically suppressed in each transition. The study situates the Western case within a comparative civilizational framework, arguing that the dual Judeo-Christian and Greco-Roman infrastructure gave Western elite factions an unusually rich toolkit for narrative appropriation — producing, as an unintended byproduct, the intellectual conditions for modern science and constitutional governance. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-permanent-reformation-elite-factions-narrative-capture-and-the-real-engine-o', 'https://archive.org/details/the-permanent-reformation-elite-factions-narrative-capture-and-the-real-engine-o', 'internet-archive:the-permanent-reformation-elite-factions-narrative-capture-and-the-real-engine-o'),
