  ('74864859059066856', 'The Ready Hand', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This study analyzes colonial independence movements across Spanish America (1808-1826), British India, French Africa, and Dutch Indonesia, arguing that independence resulted from elite opportunism rather than ideological development. It demonstrates how Creole elites—locally-born colonizers educated in metropolitan administrative systems—possessed complete governing capability but were constrained by metropolitan military power until total war (Napoleon''s invasion of Spain, World War II) removed that constraint. The analysis reveals how post-independence societies preserved existing hierarchies, with colonial-educated local elites replacing metropolitan administrators without transforming underlying power structures. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-ready-hand', 'https://archive.org/details/the-ready-hand', 'internet-archive:the-ready-hand'),
  ('1033451913063594392', 'The real cost of cheap food', 'Michael Carolan', 2011, 'Economics', 'The real cost of cheap food by Michael Carolan. A economics book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL15916422W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL15916422W-'),
