  ('1039490839484839588', 'The Reconquista, Material Power And Belief Infrastructure Competition', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This study examines the Christian reconquest of Iberia (1031-1492), analyzing how Christian kingdoms conquered Islamic Spain through military superiority, demographic advantages, and economic resources rather than theological correctness or divine favor. It demonstrates how the same territories changed religions three times (Christian→Muslim→Christian) following whoever held material power, with populations adopting the dominant religion through identical conversion mechanisms in both directions—economic incentives, social advantages, legal benefits, and pragmatic accommodation. The case reveals how Christians systematically appropriated Islamic governance mechanisms (transforming the dhimmi system into the Mudéjar system in reverse), validating that belief infrastructure serves whoever holds material power regardless of religious content. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-reconquista-material-power-and-belief-infrastructure-competition', 'https://archive.org/details/the-reconquista-material-power-and-belief-infrastructure-competition', 'internet-archive:the-reconquista-material-power-and-belief-infrastructure-competition'),
