  ('227981399789004092', 'The Revolutionary''s Dilemma, From Defensive Necessity To Elite Capture', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This study examines why revolutionary movements systematically reproduce authoritarian hierarchies by analyzing three historical cases: the Paris Commune (1871), the Haitian Revolution (1791-1804), and the Bolshevik Revolution through Stalin''s consolidation. It demonstrates how Lenin''s systematic study of revolutionary failures led him to build organizational capacity for defense against elite counterattack—centralized command, economic control, and capacity for violence—but this same apparatus proved perfectly designed for autocratic capture through Elite Darwinism. The paper introduces the Type A/B/C operator framework to explain how hierarchical structures select for strategic opportunists regardless of founders'' intentions, showing that the toolkit of liberation is structurally identical to the toolkit of domination. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-revolutionarys-dilemma-from-defensive-necessity-to-elite-capture', 'https://archive.org/details/the-revolutionarys-dilemma-from-defensive-necessity-to-elite-capture', 'internet-archive:the-revolutionarys-dilemma-from-defensive-necessity-to-elite-capture'),
