  ('429883451029403358', 'The Sacred Boundary, Kapu, Chiefly Power, And The Self Abolition Of A Belief Infrastructure In Hawaii', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'Historical Fiction', 'This case study examines the kapu system — the elaborate framework of sacred prohibitions that organized Hawaiian social life for centuries — and its deliberate abolition by royal decree in 1819, six months before the arrival of Christian missionaries. It analyzes how the mana economy, gender hierarchies within the ali''i (chiefly) class, and the kahuna interpretive class sustained elite power, and how a specific factional calculation by Ka''ahumanu and Liholiho dismantled the system from within during the succession crisis following Kamehameha I''s death. The case is treated as a laboratory study within the larger series, notable for being the only documented instance of a ruling class abolishing its own belief infrastructure before foreign replacement arrived. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-sacred-boundary-kapu-chiefly-power-and-the-self-abolition-of-a-belief-infrastructure-in-hawaii', 'https://archive.org/details/the-sacred-boundary-kapu-chiefly-power-and-the-self-abolition-of-a-belief-infrastructure-in-hawaii', 'internet-archive:the-sacred-boundary-kapu-chiefly-power-and-the-self-abolition-of-a-belief-infrastructure-in-hawaii'),
