  ('529147395194645905', 'The Seal And The Flame, Mani, Shapur I, And The Politics Of Divine Revelation In Third Century Persia, C. 216– 293 CE', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'Historical Fiction', 'This case study examines the thirty-year relationship between Mani (216–276/277 CE), the founder of Manichaeism, and Shapur I of the Sasanian Empire, analyzing how a single ruler''s military dominance created political space for religious innovation that was immediately reversed upon his death, and how the mechanism of patronage-dependency makes belief systems vulnerable to institutional capture by competing religious establishments. It traces Mani''s synthesis of Zoroastrian, Christian, and Buddhist traditions into a universal religion equipped with portable institutional architecture (the Elect-Hearer two-tier system), accessible written canon in multiple languages, and a sophisticated cosmological framework (Light imprisoned in Darkness) designed to operate across civilizational boundaries. The study demonstrates how Shapur I''s toleration of Mani derived not from spiritual conviction but from structural calculation: a competing religious network operating throughout the empire''s diverse territories could constrain the Zoroastrian priestly establishment''s institutional monopoly without threatening the king''s political dominance. It examines how the high priest Kartir spent fifty years building fire temple networks, establishing priestly hierarchies, and accumulating political capital precisely to exploit the moment when royal patronage ended, showing how Kartir''s role in installing Bahram I as king converted priestly institutional accumulation into direct control of succession, resulting in Mani''s arrest and execution around 276/277 CE and the systematic destruction of Manichaean institutional infrastructure in Persia. The study demonstrates that the five conditions for successful belief-system suppression (unified elite commitment, geographic containment, target vulnerability, overwhelming force, and multi-generational persistence) were all present in unusually pure form, explaining why suppression succeeded completely in Persia while the diaspora communities—expelled from patronage and forced to develop independent institutional infrastructure—achieved greater long-term durability, surviving for more than a millennium in North Africa, Central Asia, and China, profoundly shaping Augustine''s Christian theology through the problems Manichaean doctrine posed. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-seal-and-the-flame-mani-shapur-i-and-the-politics-of-divine-revelation-in-th', 'https://archive.org/details/the-seal-and-the-flame-mani-shapur-i-and-the-politics-of-divine-revelation-in-th', 'internet-archive:the-seal-and-the-flame-mani-shapur-i-and-the-politics-of-divine-revelation-in-th'),
  ('201020902381528582', 'The Seal And The Landscape', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This case study examines the founding of Islam through the lens of prophetic convergence, analyzing how Muhammad''s emergence in seventh-century Arabia drew on a densely layered ideological landscape — including Hanif monotheism, Jewish and Nestorian Christian communities, and the Manichaean prophetic architecture that had previously deployed the "Seal of the Prophets" title. It highlights two key mechanisms: the theological repositioning of the Abrahamic covenant through the Ishmael genealogy, which placed Arabia at the center of prophetic history rather than its periphery, and the precise correspondence between the community''s material conditions (persecution, then military capacity, then imperial expansion) and the theological content of each phase of the revelation. The study further traces how the founding architecture''s institutional products — the legal schools, the Sufi propagation networks, the waqf endowment system, and the canonical Quranic text — proved more durable than any of the political structures built upon them across fourteen centuries. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-seal-and-the-landscape', 'https://archive.org/details/the-seal-and-the-landscape', 'internet-archive:the-seal-and-the-landscape'),
