  ('48433513438351570', 'The Sedna Codex: Founding Documents of a Proposed Arctic Research Institute', 'Sum Bunny', 2026, 'Science', 'The Sedna Codex is a speculative founding document proposing an Indigenous-governed research and inquiry institute to be located in Greenland (Kalaallit Nunaat). Written as a personal offering rather than an institutional claim, the Codex is explicitly framed as a request for permission , not a plan for imposition. It integrates scientific inquiry, Kalaallit obligation, and creative catalysis into a single governance framework designed to prevent extractive research practices. The document proposes a consent-based structure—including Indigenous veto authority, data stewardship mechanisms, and refusal rights—intended to ensure that scientific work serves the land and its people rather than external interests. Artists are embedded not as communicators or decorators, but as creative catalysts within scientific teams to expand the range of inquiry itself. This text does not assert the existence of the proposed institution. Instead, it offers a model for how research, governance, and culture might be re-aligned in response to climate change, Arctic extraction pressures, and the historical misuse of Indigenous land for scientific and military purposes. Licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 (CC BY 4.0), The Sedna Codex is published as part of the ongoing Sum Bunny / Ark Hive Press canon exploring systems theory, ethics of inquiry, and non-extractive institutional design.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/sedna-codex-sum-bunny-2026_202601', 'https://archive.org/details/sedna-codex-sum-bunny-2026_202601', 'internet-archive:sedna-codex-sum-bunny-2026_202601'),
