  ('272829972206526929', 'The Soldier''s Choice, Five Moments When Military Commanders Decided The Fate Of Republics', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This comparative study examines five historical moments when military commanders were offered the chance to seize political power—Napoleon Bonaparte (1799), George Washington (1783), Adolf Hitler (1933), Smedley Butler (1933), and Belisarius (540 CE)—analyzing the structural conditions that repeatedly produce these crises and why the individual choice to accept or refuse determines outcomes measured in millions of lives. It demonstrates that elite recruitment of military strongmen follows a consistent pattern across seventeen centuries: property-owning elites facing democratic redistribution seek a controllable instrument of force, consistently underestimate the strongman''s capacity to escape their control, and suffer consequences far beyond what they intended to prevent. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-soldiers-choice-five-moments-when-military-commanders-decided-the-fate-of-republics', 'https://archive.org/details/the-soldiers-choice-five-moments-when-military-commanders-decided-the-fate-of-republics', 'internet-archive:the-soldiers-choice-five-moments-when-military-commanders-decided-the-fate-of-republics'),
