  ('1038827557960978563', 'The Spring Of Goliath', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This counterfactual history examines how the death of Möngke Khan at Diaoyu Castle in 1259 triggered a cascade of events that halted the Mongol conquest of the Islamic world and potentially saved both Islamic civilization and Europe from Mongol domination. It analyzes how Möngke''s death forced Hulagu Khan to withdraw from Syria with the bulk of his forces, enabling the Mamluk victory at Ain Jalut (1260)—the first decisive defeat of a Mongol army—and argues that this single biological contingency deflected the trajectory of multiple civilizations. The paper demonstrates how individual events and human agency, rather than structural determinism alone, shaped the survival of the Mamluk Sultanate, the continuation of Islamic institutional authority, and the preservation of the fragmented European state system that would later produce the Renaissance and Scientific Revolution. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-spring-of-goliath', 'https://archive.org/details/the-spring-of-goliath', 'internet-archive:the-spring-of-goliath'),
  ('898127997162715795', 'The Spring of the Tiger', 'Eleanor Burford', 1979, 'Romance', 'The Spring of the Tiger by Eleanor Burford. A romance book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL3931415W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL3931415W-'),
