  ('697188353758485230', 'The Sun''s Debt, Human Sacrifice, Conquest, And The Architecture Of Aztec Power', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'Historical Fiction', 'This case study examines the Aztec empire''s belief infrastructure as the series'' most explicitly documented case of ideological construction, tracing how the chief minister Tlacaelel deliberately burned existing historical records and commissioned replacements to elevate the tribal deity Huitzilopochtli to cosmic supremacy, installing the Fifth Sun mythology as the theological justification for a sacrifice and tribute extraction system operating across roughly thirty-eight provincial territories. It analyzes the sacrifice economy''s managed supply chain — including the Flower War with Tlaxcala as a deliberately maintained victim-supply arrangement — to demonstrate how the cosmological claim that the sun required continuous human blood functioned as legitimation for systematic military conquest and tribute extraction. The study uses the Spanish conquest''s rapid collapse of indigenous resistance, driven by ninety thousand allied warriors drawn from resentful tribute-paying communities, as the limit test revealing why a compliance architecture addressed to the benefit of one people rather than embedded in each subject''s personal spiritual interest fails to survive external disruption. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-suns-debt-human-sacrifice-conquest-and-the-architecture-of-aztec-power', 'https://archive.org/details/the-suns-debt-human-sacrifice-conquest-and-the-architecture-of-aztec-power', 'internet-archive:the-suns-debt-human-sacrifice-conquest-and-the-architecture-of-aztec-power'),
