  ('327985201986566579', 'The Ultimates', 'Mark Millar, Bryan Hitch, Andrew Currie', 2002, 'Graphic Novel', 'The Ultimates by Mark Millar, Bryan Hitch, Andrew Currie', 'https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/950581-L.jpg', 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL5749650W', 'internet-archive:ol-works-OL5749650W'),
  ('28087054439200466', 'The Umayyad Caliphate And Elite Power Scaling Through Belief Appropriation', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'Historical Fiction', 'This comprehensive five-part study examines the Umayyad Caliphate (661-750 CE), analyzing how a Meccan merchant elite transformed from opponents of Muhammad into rulers of one of history''s largest empires through the appropriation of Islamic belief systems. It demonstrates how belief systems function as infrastructure for power scaling—enabling a 1,000-fold multiplication of authority from regional control to an empire spanning from Spain to Central Asia—while simultaneously creating constraints that elites cannot violate without undermining their own legitimacy. The study traces the complete cycle from appropriation through the Karbala massacre''s revelation of power''s true basis in force, the seventy-year accumulation of narrative consequences, the Abbasid revolution''s strategic deployment of delegitimization narratives, and the ultimate proof of elite consciousness through the Abbasids'' immediate betrayal of Shia supporters after taking power. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-umayyad-caliphate-and-elite-power-scaling-through-belief-appropriation', 'https://archive.org/details/the-umayyad-caliphate-and-elite-power-scaling-through-belief-appropriation', 'internet-archive:the-umayyad-caliphate-and-elite-power-scaling-through-belief-appropriation'),
  ('811215457218051958', 'The Umayyad Caliphate Of Córdoba, From Conquest To Caliphate, Islamic Legitimacy In Al Andalus, 711 929', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This case study examines the Umayyad conquest and consolidation of al-Andalus (Islamic Spain, 711-929 CE), demonstrating how a Muslim minority elite governed a Christian-Jewish majority through the dhimmi system and Islamic legal frameworks. It analyzes how Abd al-Rahman I and his descendants maintained Islamic legitimacy despite condemnation from the Abbasid Caliphate in Baghdad, creating an independent emirate that eventually proclaimed itself a rival caliphate in 929 CE. The study reveals how belief infrastructure operates under maximum strain when official religious authority opposes the ruler, yet territorial control, effective governance, and systematic appropriation of Islamic legitimacy enable sustainable minority rule across two centuries. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-umayyad-caliphate-of-cordoba-from-conquest-to-caliphate-islamic-legitimacy-in-al-andalus-711-929', 'https://archive.org/details/the-umayyad-caliphate-of-cordoba-from-conquest-to-caliphate-islamic-legitimacy-in-al-andalus-711-929', 'internet-archive:the-umayyad-caliphate-of-cordoba-from-conquest-to-caliphate-islamic-legitimacy-in-al-andalus-711-929'),
