  ('1034176585757348739', 'The Voynich Manuscript In Digital Facsimile', 'M. Davis, Editor', 2025, 'Historical Fiction', 'The Voynich Manuscript is an illustrated codex hand-written in an unknown script and language. Carbon-dated to the early 15th century (1404–1438), it is named after Wilfrid Voynich, the rare book dealer who acquired it in 1912. The manuscript remains undeciphered despite extensive cryptographic and linguistic research. Its pages contain botanical drawings, astronomical and cosmological diagrams, confusing scenes, and dense pages of mysterious text. The Voynich Manuscript (Beinecke MS 408) presents one of the most enduring puzzles in manuscript studies. Comprising over 200 vellum folios, the work features a unique, undeciphered writing system and a rich visual program of plants that are nearly all unknown or unidentifiable, astronomical charts, anatomical figures, and pharmaceutical imagery. Housed at Yale University''s Beinecke Rare Book & Manuscript Library, the manuscript offers valuable insights into early modern knowledge systems, even as its origin, purpose, and language remain unknown. The manuscript continues to attract interdisciplinary interest across cryptography, linguistics, history of science, art history, and digital humanities. About This Digital Edition The Voynich Manuscript in Digital Facsimile is available here in its full, 32-bit floating point, originally captured resolution, sourced directly from Yale University''s Beinecke Library. This version is packaged in the best format I could think of suitable for all uses - CBZ format, making it readable in dedicated CBZ viewers like mcomix on GNU/Linux, as well as many others on Windows and macOS. It also serves as a direct source for the original TIFF files—ideal for scientific, archival, or high-fidelity image processing. A second edition is available in the same CBZ format, but with the color downsampled to 16 bits per channel, and most importantly in downsampled to 16-bit PNG, preserving the original resolution while adding a lot more compatibility, suitable for nearly every comic archive reader program on computer, tablet, or phone. Both CBZ files can be renamed to .ZIP (or just opened directly by most archive tools), allowing you to extract and use the images however you wish. Keep note that on most Windows version TIFF viewing natively in Windows Explorer and Photos requires the installation of a free TIFF filter in a system dialog box somewhere. And yes—there’s also a sinful A4-formatted PDF, for the rest of you, which, despite its transgressions against archival purity, looks surprisingly good, especially given its relatively small size. It does zoom in to what appears to be full resolution, but the images have obviously been JPEG lossily compressed. If you''re simply wondering how you got here and what this all is, start with the PDF. Come for the easilye scrollable mystery, stay for content that''s equally fit for scholars and the madmen alike, but mostly people in between. I did not make anything else listed in the files section, these were sort of generated automatically. OCR''ed versions of Voynich are not useful unfortunately. As are the files these things like tables of contents and page number files have sprung from. I recognize exactly 4 files you may be interested in that archive.org generated that may be of use. They''re zip files, with images in Jpeg2000 format. They''re nicer quality than the 2014 Jpeg2000 scans at higher resolution. The PNG and TIFF labeled JP2 ZIP folder are both practically identical and of the largest size, so get one of those. That leaves the last one. The torrent. Do torrent this. Do seed. Torrents usually give you and me and others way higher download speeds than I get from websites, at the expense of some upload bandwidth I wasn''t using while in bed. Of course, any license falls under Creative Commons Zero, which I believe means it''s all in the Public Domain. I only put myself as editor because, well, if anyone uses this to cite a source, they like having a name to attribute it to, so I made it easy for them. Released and Published on archive.org on 2025-04-07.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-voynich-in-digital-facsimile', 'https://archive.org/details/the-voynich-in-digital-facsimile', 'internet-archive:the-voynich-in-digital-facsimile'),
