  ('104078869389375236', 'The War For Christendom, Elite Power, Belief, And The Fracture Of Western Christendom, 1378– 1648', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'Historical Fiction', 'This comprehensive case study examines the Protestant Reformation and the fragmentation of Western Christianity from 1378 to 1648, analyzing how a challenge to the medieval Church''s monopoly on belief-based governance became an appropriation contest determined by military capacity, printing technology, and elite interests rather than theological merit. It traces the structural collapse of the Church''s legitimacy through the Great Schism (1378–1417), the challenge of early reformers, and Luther''s 1517 theses—demonstrating how Frederick the Wise''s protection, the Holy Roman Empire''s political fragmentation, and the revolutionary information environment enabled Protestantism''s survival when previous heresies had been suppressed. The study documents how German princes, the English crown, and urban elites converted Reformation theology into massive property transfers through monastery dissolution and ecclesiastical secularization, creating permanent stakeholder classes whose material interests locked in the confessional boundary. It analyzes how the resulting confessional competition produced the Thirty Years'' War (1618–1648)—one of history''s costliest conflicts measured in lives and economic devastation—and how the Peace of Westphalia formally recognized the stalemate that military force could not resolve, establishing the Protestant-Catholic boundary as durable infrastructure that structured European development for three centuries. The study demonstrates how a belief system functioning as governance infrastructure becomes resilient not through the depth of conviction it commands but through the material interests, property arrangements, and institutional structures built upon it. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-war-for-christendom-elite-power-belief-and-the-fracture-of-western-christendom-1378-1648', 'https://archive.org/details/the-war-for-christendom-elite-power-belief-and-the-fracture-of-western-christendom-1378-1648', 'internet-archive:the-war-for-christendom-elite-power-belief-and-the-fracture-of-western-christendom-1378-1648'),
  ('710807583186432058', 'The war in the air', 'H. G. Wells', 1908, 'Science Fiction', 'The war in the air by H. G. Wells. A science fiction book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL52253W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL52253W-'),
  ('609231343295916328', 'The war in the air', 'H. G. Wells', 1908, 'Science Fiction', 'The war in the air by H. G. Wells', 'https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/574890-L.jpg', 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL52253W', 'internet-archive:ol-works-OL52253W'),
  ('582025776151785412', 'The War Inside The Temple', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This theoretical essay argues that the Industrial Revolution was not primarily a technological event but the downstream consequence of a specific elite factional war conducted through theological language in Protestant Northwestern Europe — one in which the winning faction gained control of the enforcement mechanisms that define what great minds pursue, what methods are legitimate, and what questions are thinkable across generations. It identifies the structural feature distinguishing Western civilization from all other major civilizations as the dual-path inheritance created by Constantine''s synthesis: the simultaneous presence of Judeo-Christian revelation and the Greco-Roman empirical tradition as competing sources of legitimate authority, enabling Enlightenment-faction challengers to appropriate an indigenous, authoritative alternative framework unavailable to equivalent factions in Islamic or Confucian civilization, where single-path legitimating structures constrained intellectual inquiry to the questions the dominant faction endorsed. The essay then demonstrates that the Industrial Revolution required the simultaneous convergence of three conditions — intellectual space for empirical inquiry (produced by the Enlightenment faction''s partial political victory), colonial capital (produced by Atlantic slave-trade and plantation extraction), and a dispossessed proletariat (produced by parliamentary enclosure) — none of which was intentionally produced and none of which converged anywhere else in the same combination, explaining why industrial technology emerged in England and the Netherlands rather than in China, the Ottoman Empire, or India despite those civilizations'' comparable or superior capabilities in relevant domains. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-war-inside-the-temple', 'https://archive.org/details/the-war-inside-the-temple', 'internet-archive:the-war-inside-the-temple'),
  ('455531741514936407', 'The War of the Worlds', 'H. G. Wells', null, 'Fiction', 'The War of the Worlds by H. G. Wells. A fiction book.', null, 'https://openlibrary.org/works/OL52114W', 'internet-archive:ol-_works_OL52114W-'),
