  ('810093661766550191', 'The Warlord''s Dilemma Diocletian, Tokugawa Ieyasu, And The Structural Logic Of Post Crisis Stabilization', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This comparative study examines how Diocletian (Roman Empire, 284 CE) and Tokugawa Ieyasu (Japan, 1600 CE) responded to structurally identical political crises—the Third Century Crisis and Sengoku period respectively—with remarkably similar institutional solutions despite zero contact between civilizations. It analyzes the "warlord''s dilemma" of converting military victory into durable political order through sacred legitimation, administrative reorganization, legal codes regulating rivals, wealth extraction systems, and succession mechanisms, with both leaders voluntarily abdicating to prove institutions could outlive their founders. The study demonstrates why Ieyasu''s bakuhan system succeeded for 265 years while Diocletian''s Tetrarchy collapsed within two years, arguing that Rome''s scale and diversity required Constantine''s appropriation of Christianity to complete what Diocletian''s construction from raw materials could not achieve—revealing that ideological appropriation of existing belief systems proves superior to construction of new ones. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/the-warlords-dilemma-diocletian-tokugawa-ieyasu-and-the-structural-logic-of-post', 'https://archive.org/details/the-warlords-dilemma-diocletian-tokugawa-ieyasu-and-the-structural-logic-of-post', 'internet-archive:the-warlords-dilemma-diocletian-tokugawa-ieyasu-and-the-structural-logic-of-post'),
