  ('424522866894085159', 'Two Washingtons, Information Asymmetry And The American Founding Mythology', 'Thomas J. Bryan, Claude/Anthropic', 2026, 'History', 'This analysis examines George Washington through two completely different historical records—elite private correspondence revealing a calculating land speculator who coordinated secret violations of the 1763 Proclamation Line to accumulate western land holdings, versus popular mythology (Parson Weems'' fabricated "cherry tree" story and the claim Washington "cannot tell a lie") presenting him as a divinely chosen, morally perfect hero. The study demonstrates how the American Revolution was fundamentally an English Creole elite rebellion protecting material interests (land speculation, smuggling operations, political autonomy) from British oversight, requiring universal principles rhetoric to mobilize common people to fight for elite interests while believing they fought for liberty. It reveals how educational systems perpetuate information asymmetry by teaching mythology to common children while elite children learn both mythology and actual mechanisms through differential access to sources. This case study is part of the larger collection Patterns of Power: Systematic Analysis of Elite Control Mechanisms Across Civilizations , which applies an 8-part analytical framework across maximum historical variation to examine how elite power operates.', 'https://archive.org/services/img/two-washingtons-information-asymmetry-and-the-american-founding-mythology', 'https://archive.org/details/two-washingtons-information-asymmetry-and-the-american-founding-mythology', 'internet-archive:two-washingtons-information-asymmetry-and-the-american-founding-mythology'),
