  ('1015773343234898047', 'Walking amongst the turkeys 1962', 'Jeffrey Hunt', 2026, 'History', 'This group of color photographs comes from a family roll of film developed in November 1962, though the images were likely taken over a span of time rather than all at once. The first four photographs appear to show a carnival or county fair, likely sometime in late summer or early fall. Most of the roll was taken at the Schmitz turkey farm, which I believe was in or near Oregon City. The final two photographs show a rattlesnake shot by my father on a fishing trip to Klickitat. Locations, sequence, and identifications are based on family memory and remain approximate. Notes: “Here I am at six years old, walking amongst the turkeys. It seems completely irresponsible now. My gramma really did turn me loose in a field full of them. Those large birds can be dangerous, and yet there I was, moving right through the flock as if it were the most natural thing in the world. What makes the memory especially vivid is what I can still recall so clearly: those turkeys in the distance, all running toward me. Even now, looking at this photograph, I can feel that moment again. It was not just a child standing in a field. It was a child suddenly realizing that a great many birds had taken notice and were keenly interested in my every move. Let alone the gobbles. Seeing the picture now, I cannot help thinking of it in almost mythic terms, as though I were wearing some strange mantle and wandering through the domain of a kind of turkey god. But the memory is entirely real.” “Grandma took that picture, I think at the Multnomah County Fair, though I am not completely sure. It may have been in Gresham, or possibly Oregon City. On that same roll of film, we evidently went to visit the Schmitz and their turkey farm, and for some reason I do remember them living in Oregon City. One of the other pictures shows a carnival ride. I remember riding it with her. I was on the outside, and she was on the inside. I remember getting smashed hard against the side of the little car. She felt terrible about it afterward. She had been so excited to go on the ride with me, and then realized I was pinned against the side. She even took a picture of it. I have two photos of the ride without us on it. Seeing that ride again really jogged the memory. It brought the whole thing back. And then, looking at the picture, it is almost like saying, “Grandma, what were you thinking?” But in a funny way, it also feels like she somehow knew it would be worth remembering.” “New photos are found every day. This is the snake shot on Dad’s fishing trip to Klickitat. Story was that he drove up there, started on the path down the hill to the river, and this was waiting for him. Of course, shoot it in the head, get back in the car, and drive home. Fishin’ done for the day. 😊 The rest of the roll has me at a turkey farm in Oregon City.”', 'https://archive.org/services/img/jeff-and-blanche-turkey-farm-1962', 'https://archive.org/details/jeff-and-blanche-turkey-farm-1962', 'internet-archive:jeff-and-blanche-turkey-farm-1962'),
