  ('985797247562431314', 'Women In The Bhakti Movement Of 16th Century Bengal', 'Ajay Kumar Dey', 2026, 'History', 'Title: Women in the Bhakti Movement of 16th-Century Bengal: Theology, Agency, and the Gaudiya Vaishnava Tradition Author: Ajay Kumar Dey (Pranavanandan) ORCID iD: 0009-0009-1046-3555 Publisher: Gour Gadadhara, Bharatpur, Murshidabad Publication Date: March 19, 2026 Language: English Abstract / Summary This research monograph investigates the multifaceted roles and "paradoxical position" of women within the formative century of the Gaudiya Vaishnava movement in Bengal. Historically, the Bhakti movement is noted for its egalitarian potential; however, the specific mechanisms of female agency have often remained under-researched in mainstream historiography. By analyzing seminal 16th-century hagiographical and theological texts—including the Chaitanya Charitamrita , Chaitanya Bhagavata , and the lyrical Vaishnava Padavali —this study explores how women negotiated patriarchal social structures through affective devotion ( prema-bhakti ) and sacred performance. Key highlights of the study include: Critical Case Studies: An analysis of pivotal female figures, most notably Jahnava Devi , and their recognition as diksha-gurus (initiating spiritual masters). Theological Agency: How the internal logic of Gaudiya Vaishnava theology provided a unique space for female spiritual authority. Regional Focus: Insights into the religious landscape of 16th-century Bengal, with specific reference to the traditions maintained in Sripat Bharatpur, Murshidabad . Comparative Visibility: A brief comparative look at female saints across other South Asian Bhakti branches (Alvars, Virashaivas, etc.).', 'https://archive.org/services/img/women-in-the-bhakti-movement-of-16th-century-bengal', 'https://archive.org/details/women-in-the-bhakti-movement-of-16th-century-bengal', 'internet-archive:women-in-the-bhakti-movement-of-16th-century-bengal'),
