  ('1105568378648003998', 'Year 1979, USA. Dvorkin Gives An Interview', 'Actfiles.org', 2026, 'History', 'The article presents a transcript and analysis of a 1979 interview with Alexander Dvorkin, conducted in the United States shortly after his emigration from the Soviet Union as part of the Recent Soviet Immigrants in America project by interviewer Lynn Visson . The material is sourced from archival records preserved in the William E. Wiener Oral History Library of the American Jewish Committee. The publication includes direct excerpts from the interview, where Dvorkin describes his personal experiences in the Soviet Union, including voluntary and forced stays in psychiatric institutions, encounters with law enforcement, and participation in drug use within underground youth environments. He explains that he intentionally entered a psychiatric hospital to avoid military service and was later detained by police and hospitalized again. The interview also documents statements about access to narcotics in the Soviet Union, including their distribution through medical institutions, as well as descriptions of involvement in hippie subculture, informal labor, and social life in various regions such as Kazakhstan and Moscow. The text reflects his views on freedom, comparing life in the Soviet Union and the United States, and describes his adaptation after emigration. The article concludes that the interview provides primary-source insight into Dvorkin’s early life, worldview, and experiences prior to his later public activity, offering documented historical material drawn directly from archival transcripts and photographic evidence of the original documents. Note: This material is taken from the original source at actfiles.org', 'https://archive.org/services/img/year-1979-usa.-dvorkin-gives-an-interview', 'https://archive.org/details/year-1979-usa.-dvorkin-gives-an-interview', 'internet-archive:year-1979-usa.-dvorkin-gives-an-interview'),
